The Digital Unix FAQ says:

> newfs is smart enough to get the geometry of the disk from the drive,
> although this feature is not documented for all versions. Use

> 	newfs /dev/rrz#x /dev/rrz#x

> to do this.

> For most uses, you don't need a disktab entry on Digital UNIX.  The disklabel
> command can get the default partition table and geometry from the disk
> driver and will put that in the label.  When the label is present, newfs
> doesn't need a disktab entry either.

However, this doesn't work with logical disks created by the LSM.

    # volassist -U fsgen make testdsk 100m
    # ls -l /dev/rvol/testdsk
    crw-------	1 root		system	40, 13 Dec 30 16:00 /dev/rvol/testdsk
    # newfs /dev/rvol/testdsk
    newfs: /dev/rvol/testdsk : only 0 partitions
    # newfs /dev/rvol/testdsk /dev/rvol/testdsk
    newfs: /dev/rvol/testdsk : only 0 partitions
    # newfs /dev/rvol/testdsk rzxx
    newfs: /dev/rvol/testdsk : only 0 partitions
    # newfs /dev/rvol/testdsk rzXX
    newfs: /dev/rvol/testdsk : only 0 partitions

The only way I can get around the silly `0 partitions' message is by
giving a bogus disk_type argument, like this:

    # newfs /dev/rvol/testdsk rz28

Is there a better way?

-- 
