In article <qxpiusp3wnn.fsf@foobar.cs.colorado.edu>, Dirk Grunwald <grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu> writes:
> "Ant Moore" <AntMoore@btinternet.com.nospam> writes:
>> Optimization doesn't have anything to do with "non-standard" coding
>> practices. If the code is legal (in that it compiles) then the only
> ---
> 
> This is the root of your problem. Just because code compiles doesn't
> mean it's 'legal'. Optimization can expose errors in your code due to
> code movement, use of temporaries, differences in the memory layout --
> any number of things. If your code is 'correct', as opposed to
> 'legal', these shouldn't matter.

My favorite VMS FORTRAN compiler bug (circa VMS 3.0) went something like this.
I was reading a file into an array using both an END= clause and an implied
do loop. I'm going to totally botch the syntax (it's been a _long_ time since
I last used an implied do loop, and I always had to look them up in the manual
anyway):

	READ(mumble,END=100)Booger(I),I=1,512

Then I wanted to look at I to see how much data was read.

The compiler generated code which:

	- Moved 512 to I
	- Ran the implied DO loop with a register for the counter

so that when the END= fired, I was always 512 regardless of how many items
had been read. I had to compile that particular subroutine /NOOPTIMIZE to
get it to work right.

AFAIK the code is legal, although perhaps a bit unusual...
