The EtherPCI LAN Card from LINKSYS uses the, "DEC PCI Ethernet DECchip 
21041," driver in Windows NT v4.0 for my DEC Alpha 21164PC system from 
Microway instead of the, "AMD PCNET Family Ethernet Adapter," driver 
shown in the Windows NT Hardware Compatibility List.  The EtherPCI LAN 
Card is a 10 Mbps card with both thin coax and 10baseT connectors.  
First, when the Alpha system started up after the card was installed, 
I had to press ESC to bypass the network initialization and press F2 
to get into the AlphaBIOS setup to disable the network initialization, 
because the computer tried to connect to a TCP/IP network, which I do 
not have.  My computer just locked up if I tried the, "AMD PCNET 
Family Ethernet Adapter," driver, and I had to resort to resetting my 
computer and pressing the spacebar as the OSLOADER started, then 
pressing L for the last known good hardware configuration, and then 
pressing ENTER to be able to log in again.  The box of the ethernet 
card says, "Combo EtherPCI LAN Card," with a picture of the card which 
clearly shows a chip with, "digital 21041-AA," printed on the chip.  
When the computer needed to load drivers from the Windows NT 
Workstation CDROM, the F:\Alpha\ directory had to be specified (F: is 
the drive letter for my CDROM drive).  I later disabled the WINS and 
TCP/IP Bindings for the adapter in the Control Panel/Network tabbed 
dialog.  The EtherPCI LAN Card is apparently a discontinued card, 
because I had to visit three different stores before finding a pair at 
CompUSA while finding plenty of EtherPCI LAN Card II cards.
 
I also purchased the LINKSYS, "Network in a Box For 32-bit PCI PCs." 
This package contains the successor to the EtherPCI LAN Card, called 
the EtherPCI LAN Card II, which is a cheaper non-bus-mastering card 
which I did not get to work in my Alpha system.  I did get the 
EtherPCI LAN Card II working in a Windows for Workgroups v3.11 
computer and a Windows 95 computer.  I was able to map an HP SureStore 
7110e CD-RW drive as a network drive in File Manager and use the XCOPY 
command to back up my Windows for Workgroups v3.11 computer over the 
thin coax cable onto the Windows 95 computer, which was hooked up to 
the HP SureStore 7110e drive through a $20 EPP parallel port card 
manufactured by SIIG.  You can designate the HP SureStore 7110e as 
shareable in Windows 95 by clicking on the My Computer icon and then 
right-clicking on the icon for the HP SureStore 7110e and then 
clicking on Properties and then changing the appropriate options.  The 
HP SureStore 7110e is a 6x read and 2x write CD-R and CD-RW drive, but 
the default setting is to do a 2x read in the properties box for some 
reason, which I changed to 6x read.  I was also able to map the HP 
SureStore 7110e as a network drive onto my my DEC Alpha Windows NT 
v4.0 computer with Explorer and do a file backup onto the HP SureStore 
7110e drive with the
 
     xcopy c:\*.* g: /m/e/c/h/k
 
command from the MSDOS prompt of the Alpha computer.  G: was the drive 
letter assigned to the networked HP SureStore 7110e drive on my Alpha 
computer.  The /m option makes xcopy only copy the files with the 
archive attribute set and makes xcopy unset the archive attribute when 
the copy is complete.  The /e option makes xcopy copy even empty 
subdirectories.  The possible consequences of not copying empty 
subdirectories are a lot confusion if you ever need to do an emergency 
restoration and do not get the \TEMP directory because it was empty at 
the time that the backup was made.  Not having the \TEMP directory 
will potentially mess up software installations and make a lot of 
programs that create temporary files malfunction.  The /c option makes 
xcopy continue onto the next file if an error occurs, such as when 
xcopy tries to copy the pagefile.sys file, which is in use at the time 
that the xcopy command tries to process the file, which you would not 
want to be copied anyway.  The /h option makes xcopy copy hidden 
files.  The /k option makes the copied files keep their original file 
attributes, such as the read-only attribute.
 
I am not able to see the Alpha computer or its files over the network 
from either my Windows for Workgroups computer or my Windows 95 
computer, but I am able to see the files for the Windows for 
Workgroups computer and Windows 95 computer from my Alpha system.  The 
Windows for Workgroups computer and Windows 95 computer are able to 
see each other's files.
 
I hope that someone else finds this information useful, because I 
certainly found the ability to share storage devices across different 
computers useful.  Among my three computers, the drivers for the HP 
SureStore 7110e is available only on the Windows 95 computer.  FX!32 
did not get the Windows NT drivers for the HP SureStore 7110e working 
on my Alpha system.  Getting a backup of the Alpha Windows NT software 
was essential, since Service Pack 3 had been applied to the operating 
system, but the Windows NT Workstation CD that came with my computer 
did not contain the service pack of bugfixes, as I found out when I 
activated dial-up networking.  Using a 10Mbps network card to transfer 
files between computers is certainly faster than transferring files 
with floppy disks or Zip drives.
 
 
-Ed L



