On the DECpc AXP 150, the Compaq QVision was the only card supported for a
long time. The ARC console has code that initializes the card on Power-up.

There is an ECU for OpenVMS and UNIX and a second, different ECU for
Windows NT. Each time you switch from one Operating System to another, you
must re-run the appropriate ECU for the OS you intend to boot and use.
This behaviour still stands today. The reason for this is that one of the
fundemental differences between Operating Systems is how they handle
interupts. Couple this knowledge with the mechanism that Microsoft used to
insulate the OS from hardware differences, called the HAL code. DIGITAL
used a different mechanism to insulate the hardware from the different
OS's, it's called the PAL code. So, you do not have broken card. There
was, at one time a native driver for the QVision, for Windows NT V3.1 and
even V3.51. Now, I do not believe that Microsoft is maintinaing the driver
any more with future releases of Windows NT, thus, you end up with a
Generic VGA driver. If you wish, go to the Display Control Panel and
Change Adapter, see what choices you get. You may be wonderfully
surprised.

As a general rule of thumb for all Windows NT users, be they Alpha or
Intel, normal Microsoft convention is to install a GENERIC DRIVER. If you
want to install and use a higher performance NATIVE driver, you must take
manual steps to do that yourself as described above. One cannot normally
install a NATIVE driver during the initial setup of Windows NT.

I regard this as a limitation in the Microsoft Installation architecture.
I don't think they care either. Same goes for Tape Drives, except here it
gets fun. Say you install NT, then the Tape driver, then upgrade, you have
a working tape drive. Say you install NT, then upgrade, then install the
Tape driver. again a working tape drive. Unfortunately, those two systems
wil not be able to interchange tapes as one of them has a newer driver
based on when the Service Pack was installed. Another fine mushy event
from Microsoft.

rtt




