>The command prompt only understands 8.3 file names and will truncate stuff
>and also delete stuff, unbeknownst to the innocent.
>[....]

The Windows NT command prompt does understand long file names.  When
you need to change the directory, for example, you have to enclose a
long directory name in double quotes or use a wildcard.  When you want to
copy a long file name, you have to enclose the file name in double
quotes or use a wildcard.

I was concerned about the possibility of file name truncation, but found
to my satisfaction that no file name truncation occurred.  What I
previously described was backing up Windows NT v4.0 files on an
Alpha 21164PC computer to an HP SureStore 7110e CD-R/CD-RW drive on a
Windows 95 computer running on a Cyrix P166 processor based computer.
After the backup was complete, I listed the files that were on the
CD-RW (rewriteable) disk while I was on the Windows 95 system, and the
long file names were there.

The HP SureStore 7110e drive uses a non-standard file system which
preserves long file names.  The drivers take care of the interface
with the operating system.  The drive is also capable of creating
regular ISO 9660 compatible CD-R disks, but you must buy separate
third-party software to do so.  I have read articles where people
mentioned the EZ CD Creator software from Corel.

-Ed L



