Unfortunately, Mr. Minh H. Cao, this is not the place to learn about VAX
clusters. The best palce to start is with some DIGITAL manuals, how about
some OpenVMS manuals. If oyu have access to the CD-ROM documentation, then
by all means start reading.

VAX clusters or Alpha clusters are really quite simple. They are a
collection of computers running from a single copy of the Operating
System. So, a little old MicroVAX 2000 and a great big old VAX 7000 and a
moderate sized VAX 4000 model 700 can all be running from a single copy of
the OS. Each machine cooperates with all the others in offering the sum
total of their resources available to the user community. This is truly
scalable processing at it's finest implimentation. Need just a little more
horspower, add a little MicroVAX. Need lots of extra grunt, add a big VAX.
The communication path between the machines was via a number of things, CI
(Cluster Interconnect) being one, DSSI (DIGITAL Storage Systems
Interconnect) another, Ethernet being another, FDDI, and I believe today
that SCSI works in some places. When A user needed to log in, he used a
name for the cluster, and the cluster would place the user onto the
machine with the most power availabel at that time. This basically
balanced the laod on the cluster. It also menat that when a machine is
shut down for service, nobody knows but the IS people that a machine is
momentarily off the air. This mechanism also facilitates rolling upgrades,
that is, one can upgrade cluster members, and reboot them over time,
affording a minimal or maybe even no impact to the user base. I won't go
on, read some manuals, it's all in there.

rtt

