In article <01bd0fda$19517d60$71689cce@moinm.mho.net>, "Moin" <moinm@mho.net> writes:
:I have A Delni-BA, 
:rev E02, 
:ECO 08, 
:MFG AS  042
:
:I need to know what this is & where can I use it.

  The DELNI is a device that eliminates the need for Ethernet cabling;
  it is an electronic emulation of a spool of Ethernet cable in a box.

  The DELNI makes a nice terminal stand, a great plant stand, a wonderful
  conversation piece from the early Ethernet age, and it could also be
  used as the hub of a small Ethernet network.

  The DELNI uses the AUI slidelock connection, which not many Ethernet
  controllers use anymore.  DELNIs can be ganged together to two levels
  -- up to 64 stations are possible this way -- and DELNIs can also be
  connected to an H400{0,5}-series Ethernet transceiver and some Ethernet
  cabling, allowing one to effectively connect up to eight Ethernet stations
  to one Ethernet transceiver.

  There are a total of eight AUI connections of one gender, and a "local"
  switch that enables or disables the ninth AUI connection (of the other
  gender).  The ninth AUI connection is routed to the transceiver or to
  a port on the upper-level DELNI.  (A maximum of two DELNIs or a DELNI
  and a transceiver can be connected together.  Two "heirarchical" DELNIs
  and a H400x transciever, or three levels of heirarchical" DELNIs are not
  permitted.  Four DELNIs is right out.)

