> >version.
> 
> We are running one of these guys on our Alphaserver 2100 4/275 at VMS V6.2.
> 
> Several notes:
> 
>   1) There's a 10mbs device you can't get rid of already in the box, so your
>      new NIC will be an add-on, not a replacement.  You get to tell all
of your
>      networking protocols about this in different ways.
> 
>   2) The device is EWB0.  To make LAT use it, you need to 
> 
>      $ DEFINE/SYSTEM LAT$DEVICE EWB0
> 
>      before doing the LAT startup.
> 
>   3) To DECnet, you'll have to refer to the device as EWA-1.  (This was 
>      definitely not obvious to me, and I had to ask about it here.)
> 
>   4) We use Multinet.  For Multinet, we had to define it as a new interface
>      (SE1), and turn it on using MULTINET CONFIG/INTERFACE.  I don't know what
>      IP software you use.
> 
>   5) The card autosensed that it was on a 100bms circuit, which was good.
>      However, it assumes that it's on a half-duplex circuit, and you have
>      to tell it if you want it to go to full-duplex.  Run LANCP from the
>      DCL prompt and use, I think, the SET DEVICE command.
>      ($ MC LANCP
>       LANCP> HELP
>      should get you started.)
> 
> If you're at half-duplex on a full-duplex circuit, you get all kinds of
> alignment errors, framing problems, short packets, etc.  Since we got all this
> sorted out (about three weeks ago) we've had no problems with it.
> 
> Good luck!
> 
> -- Alan        
> 
