Mr. Fun <gribble@primenet.com> wrote:
>I have 100's of
>Floppy disks with data on them that were written to on a DEC PDP11/83
>running RT-11 OS.  I think the disks are formated to about 1.2 Meg.
>Do you know of any program that will allow these disks to be read in
>an IBM PC floppy disk drive?
>I don't recall the exact floppy disk format that was used, seems like
>there was two possible kinds, RX-50 and something else.  We use
>regular IBM PC HD (1.2 meg) floppies and my memory says they format
>out to 2400 blocks.  If that's critical I can find out next week.

All correct, 1.2 MB PC disks work in an RX33 drive and yield 2400 blocks.
You can read them from DOS with PUTR, "ftp://ftp.dbit.com/pub/putr/putr.com".
(the doc file is at ... /putr.doc).  Use these commands (assuming the PC's
1.2 MB drive is called "B:"):

C:\>putr
(PUTR version banner)
(C:\)>                        (this is PUTR's prompt)
(C:\)>mount b: /rt11 /rx33    (or just "mount b:" with the disk already in the
                               drive, PUTR will auto-detect type/format)
(C:\)>set copy binary         (if you need binary files, i.e. NULs intact --
                               omit this command for text files, or if you have
                               some of each then omit this command and use
                               COPY/B for copying binary files, COPY for text)
(C:\)>copy b:*.* c:foo\       (or wherever you want to put the files, N.B. if
                               you use a DOS directory name you must put the
                               backslash on the end so PUTR will know it's a
                               dir name and not a file)
[... swap in each disk and copy all the files off with more COPY commands, you
 don't need to issue a new MOUNT command for each if they're all RT-11 RX33s]
(C:\)>quit
C:\>

See the doc file for more information, including how to access RT-11 V5.x
subdisks (PUTR makes them look like subdirectories).

John Wilson
D Bit



