From: edlee@chinet.chinet.com (Edward Lee)
Subject: Robert Harley's ECDLP-89 program
Date: 1997/12/26
Message-ID: <6823ss$61h$1@chinet.chinet.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 310460514
Organization: Chinet
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


If you are running or are considering running Robert Harley's ECDLP-89 
program at full speed on your Alpha computer and are interested in milking 
the last bit of performance out of your computer, then you might consider 
using the -s compile switch to strip out the debugging information from the 
executable.  The theory is that stripping out the debugging information 
will decrease the memory footprint of the ecdlp program and make it more 
likely that more of the ecdlp program will fit within the level 1 on-chip 
processor cache of the Alpha chip.  Robert's example for compiling the 
ecdlp program is:
 
     gcc -O4 ecdlp-89.c -o ecdl -DFROM=somebody@somewhere
 
Here is my suggested change:
 
     gcc -O4 -s ecdlp-89.c -o ecdl -DFROM=somebody@somewhere
 
or
 
     gcc -O4 -s ecdlp-89.c -o ecdl -DBATCH -DFROM=somebody@somewhere
 
if your Alpha computer is not directly connected to the Internet.
 
I am using a 533Mhz 21164PC processor which has a 16KB direct-mapped L1 
cache on-chip and am getting about 311,625 iterations per second.  This is 
an increase of about 8,100 iterations per second with the -s switch, which 
works out to about 699,840,000 extra iterations per day for my computer 
alone.  I notice that my computer is creeping past other individual's 
computers on the counter list.  People who have Alpha processors with more 
than 16KB of L1 cache may not notice a difference.
 
I sent an email message to Robert Harley suggesting that he change the 
references to puts("...") to printf("...\n") in the ecdlp-89.c program, 
since printf() is already necessary for the main output of the program and 
contains the functionality of puts().  Having both functions in the program 
unnecessarily increases the size of the executable and potentially slows 
the program down by pushing more speed-critical code out of L1 cache.
 
I am running the ecdlp program with a command similar to the following:
 
     nice -n -20 ./ecdl >log.txt
 
I periodically log in to a different session by pressing ALT F2, in order 
to execute mcopy to copy the log.txt file to a floppy disk, log out, and 
then press ALT F1 to go back to the number-crunching session.
 
I have also executed renice to lower the priorities of other non-essential 
processes in the system.
 
-Ed L
 
If you are interested in joining the challenge, see:
 
http://www.certicom.com/chal/contents.htm
http://pauillac.inria.fr/~harley/ecdl2/
 
I had to get the ecdlp-89.c program from the mirror site that is mentioned 
in a ReadMe file at Robert's site.



