In article <01bd0bec$ceffcd20$a095e5cf@tom>,
dave chlopecki <david@topelforman.com> wrote:
>I am at a office that has been using a digital 486 EISA Server and as soon
>as I put Windows 95 machines on the network, the server and all its clients
>run slowly. 
>I am running Netware 3.12 and have loaded the 3.12 patches.

Disable the ability to be a Browse Master on 90% of your Win95 machines.
Remove all unneeded protocols from them, too (Win95 will broadcast any
packet it sends out the NIC in each protocol, whether it needs to or
not, so if you have three protocols configured, you get three packets
on the wire for every one your software generates).

You can improve client response a bit by rearranging the order of the
protocol bindings so that the one you're likely to use most often is
first in the list. That way, you at least get the necessary packet out
faster, and will receive it sooner as far as the application is concerned.

<sarcasm> Trust me. I'm a Microsoft Certified Professional, a Product
Specialist for Windows 95 </sarcasm>. (Meaning I passed two of the
tests on the way to an MCSE; really, I didn't learn that much about
Win95 other than it doesn't network as well as Unix stuff. But the 
above should be helpful--I'd be more precise, but the Win95 Resource 
Kit is at work.)


Gary

