In article <67eef5$7ei$1@thefuture.qualcomm.com>,
Ryan Moore <rmoore@qualcomm.com> wrote:
>Here's an example of a difference:
>
>#include <stdio.h>
>
>main()
>{
>        char bar[50];
>
>        printf("1: ");
>        scanf("%s", bar);
>
>        fflush(stdin);

I'm afraid that doing an output flush operation on an input stream
results in undefined behavior. Undefined behavior can easily be responsible
for one compiler behaving differently from another. Allowing fflush()
on stdin is an extension that some systems support as a non-portable
way of throwing away data that has been queued up in the input buffer.


>        printf("2: ");

You should be doing fflush(stdout) to ensure that the output appears
prior to the expectation of input from the user.

>        scanf("%c", bar);
>}
>
>On VAX C, the second scanf() will wait until the user enters a character 
>followed by the enter key.  On DEC C, the second scanf() will *not* wait for 
>the user to enter a character.

The %c conversion specifier causes the next character in the input stream
to be dequeued whether or not it is a whitespace character ot what
have you. Unlike with %s, no skipping of leading whitespace occurs. So if
there is an unremoved newline character in the input stream, it is becomes
converted.

It could be that one compiler's library supports fflush(stdin) as a way
of throwing away that extra newline.  Since the behavior is undefined,
a compiler may do anything it pleases, such as provide an extension,
arrange for the program to die, etc.

Try it with both compilers without the fflush(stdin) and post your results.



