George Macomber wrote:
> 
> What about a celebris gt 5133 can I replace the 133 chip with a
> 200 chip and will I need to change any jumpers.Also
> will it be a signeficant boost in performance

I've put CD-ROMs in a couple of those but that is all -
my cpu overclockings have only been with the Venturis line.

However, with a P133 already in the computer I 
wouldn't bother with upgrading it - I would just
overclock it to 166 MHz.  The speed difference 
between 166 MHz and 200 MHz is not enough to make
it worth buying a new cpu. Appendix A in your manual 
will tell you which jumpers to use to change the 
bus-to-cpu-core multiplier from 2 to 2.5.  If 
this fries your cpu (just a slim possibility when 
overclocking by that little bit), then fall back
on the cpu replacement idea.

Your motherboard definitely has the jumpers to
set your multiplier to the 3 that a P200 needs
(3 * 66.67 MHz = 200 MHz).  The tricky part lies
in getting the voltage right for the cpu, and 
Digital's manuals are consistently skimpy in
explaining how to do this.  However, I have
heard from some people that the P166,P200,P166 MMX 
and P200 MMX (which are all 2.8 volt cpu's) all
have a voltage regulator built into them so that if you
plug them into a 3.3 volt socket (such as what your
P133 is sitting in right now) you will be fine.

Also keep in mind the need for extra cooling when
you upgrade or replace your cpu.  This is especially
true for Compaq's and Digital's low-profile desktops
where you have the same hardware heating half as much
air as in a mid-tower but with a smaller power supply
and cooling fan.

Rob



