Nigel Arnot wrote:
> Some of these errors are fundamentally undiagnosable by a compiler.
> For example, suppose you have a routine in a DLL to zero out an
> array, someinthing like
> 
>    void zero( char *a, int n) { while (n>0) { *a++ = 0; --n; }}
> 
> (out of my head, so assume it's correct even if it isn't)
> 
> and it gets called by something you are compiling that does
> 
>    zero( mystuff, m*4)
> 
> There's absolutely no way to check when the DLL was compiled that
> mystuff really is a pointer to m*4 chars, and there's no way for
> what you are compiling to know that m*4 is supposed to be the size
> of what mystuff is pointing at. So if you've made an error and
> mystuff is pointing to the wrong place in memory or m has the
> wrong value, something may get zeroed that shouldn't have been.
> 
> In these circumstances it's quite probable that optimized and
> non-optimized code will behave differently. It's my experience
> that one more commonly sees a non-optimized version that "works"
> become an optimized one that doesn't. This probably simply
> reflects the fact that most programmers debug non-optimized
> versions first, because symbolic debuggers work best that way,
> and so if the bug had disrupted operation of the non-optimized
> version they'd have immediately looked for a programming error.
> If, however, something that has "worked" for months breaks when
> optimised, they tend to want to blame the optimizer. That's just
> human nature.

True. But, sometimes the optimizer really is wrong. It can have a bug.
It can have a bug that only I have found. It's possible, and has
happened on occasion.

-- 
