Bob DeBula wrote:
> 
>  2.) A rather simple interface and rather easy system administration
>      for the non Unix saavy user (seemingly fewer calls from newbie SGI
>      purchasers than most other popular Unix vendor box purchasers).

Not true of Digital UNIX which has a full suite of GUI installation and
management tools (except for TruCluser, but that's coming). Expect some
major enhancements in this area when V5.0 ships too.  Those of you who
have been to UNIX Symposium's in the last year will know what I'm talking
about.

What disappointed me most about SGI was their noticable absence from
the CDE roll out.  Now non-Unix saavy users can move between AIX, HP-UX,
Solaris, Digital UNIX, *and* VMS systems without having to retrain or
reskill.  Not so with SGI. 


>  4.) Hardware phase-outs (due to OS support dropping) have been rather
>      gradual processes thus far (no DECStation/Ultrix to Alpha/OSF1
>      type transitions).

I'd like to add some public facts (not personal opinion) to this point:

 - DEC OSF/1 (pre-UNIX branding) V1.2 was released in March 1993, the
   Ultrix port to Alpha was retired at that time.  That's 4 years and
   9 (ish) months ago.  

 - Ultrix/MIPS continued.  The last release V4.5 shipped (according to
   the date on the SPD) in February 1996, 2 years 11 months later.

 - Ultrix/MIPS was retired in April 1996, 3 years and 1 month later, at
   which point you could not buy new versions,

 - Despite this, Ultrix Operating System support continues even today, and
   though it's rare, bug reports are still accepted and patches continue
   to be produced.  For example the latest patch kit for V4.5 was issued on
   29th September 1997.

 - You can still log calls at your local CSC on Ultrix today! (contract 
   required of cause).

 - Ultrix support is guaranteed until at least the end of 1999. (I won't
   speculate on what happens after that). 

