In article <676ndb$47o@usenet.srv.cis.pitt.edu>, geest3+@pitt.edu (Gregg E Economou) writes:
:Is there any equivalent to the T 50 command on a uv2000, on the microVAX II? 
:How else would one go about seeing what's where on the bus on 
:a microvax II? Do i need the (hard to find) field diagnostic tape? 

  Ah, software is the approach used by a software engineer -- hardware
  engineers go for the screwdriver and crack open the box and look.  :-)

  Seriously -- on the MicroVAX II series and similar older systems -- the
  easiest way to see what's on the Q-bus (or UNIBUS) is to open up the
  card cage and look for the codes on the ends of the cards, and/or the
  name of the module in the module etch.  On the Q-bus, DIGITAL modules
  have an "M" codenumber stamped on them, and this can be translated into
  the module identification.

  In the case of the Q-bus, you _will_ have to check the individual CSR
  and interrupt vector settings, as host software generally has no (easy)
  way of determining the interrupt vector setting on a particular module.
  This usually means you will need the manual for each module in the system.
  Also remember that the Q-bus CSR settings "cascade" -- if you insert or
  remove a Q-bus module, you will have to determine if other module CSR
  settings (and/or interrupt vector settings) must be changed.  Q-bus
  modules do not exist in isolation -- each module tends to affects the
  setting of the next module in the configuration priority order.  (Yes,
  there are a few select Q-bus modules that have fixed CSR addresses.)

  With the MicroVAX 2000 and VAXstation 2000, there isn't really an I/O
  expansion bus, and there are very few modules that can be installed.
  One can usually tell what is present in the configuration based on the
  ROM diagnostics.

  --

  If you are working on older systems, and supporting the hardware, I
  would recommend joining the "DIGITAL Assisted Services" (DAS) program.
  DAS -- sometimes called DECmailer -- gives you a way to get modules
  swapped, and gives you a way to gain access to the service tools and
  diagnostics you might need.

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
