In article <6751pj$265@maroon.tc.umn.edu>, mari0021@tc.umn.edu writes:
:the 'uname -m' command  gives the serial number of the CPU
:on an AIX but not on the Alpha running Digital UNIX.
:
:Does anyone know how to get the unique CPU serial number/id on the Alpha 1000?
:I've searched the entire documentation CD, but the closest I got was the
:'uerf' command run in superuser mode which gives a 'system id' among other
:things, and 'pset_info' which only shows the CPU number (that is, 0 for 1 cpu).

  Most Alpha and most VAX systems do not have "solid" serial numbers -- and
  those few systems that do have something that vaguely resembles a serial
  number can sometimes have that value changed via console commands or
  backplane switchpacks.

  For something that can be used in lieu of a serial number, consider the
  Ethernet Address.   For details and example C code, see the Ethernet
  section at the URL:

  http://www.partner.digital.com/www-swdev/pages/Home/TECH/faqs/ovms/ovms.html

  The serial number check is something that tends to be used primarily in
  software licensing schemes, and is something that tends to regularly get
  mis-implemented.  I would tend to recommend using an existing licensing
  package if at all possible, as this puts the headaches on somebody else.

  Many folks try to use the entire SID register as a serial number, and it
  doesn't work that way -- one can only depend on the architected portion
  of the SID, the processor-dependent portion can -- and does -- vary.

  On the Alpha SRM console (used by OpenVMS and UNIX), the hardware restart
  parameter block (HWRPB) is another option.  Look at the ten ASCII character
  field beginning at offset 64.  (Donno how to get at the console HWRPB from
  UNIX, however.)  And I'd use the Ethernet address first...

