Arun Sharma (asharma@this.is.not.me.netscape.com) wrote:
: On Wed, 17 Dec 1997 21:38:48 GMT, Zalman Stern wrote:

: 	z> Dubious economics.

: Hence, no company in the world other than Intel will ever try to build
: a processor because it's going to lose the volume game ?

In the near term, this may be the case. Certainly for new general purpose
architectures. E.g. Alpha, the architecture for the next 25 years has a
bleak future 7 years in due to economics. (Mostly the expense of fab lines,
but supporting an architecture well isn't at all cheap either.)

There will always be new architecture development on the highend and in the
embedded market. Some profitable, some not. But development of complete new
instruction sets will be less prevalent as time goes on. Which is fine by
me so long as we have a few good ones to work with. There's plenty of other
interesting work to do.

: 	z>  Patent law.

: What's patented ? The instruction set ? No one can patent a
: philosophy. Nobody patented RISC.

Certainly various techniques used in the implementation of RISC
architectures were patented. MIPS held patents on the standard biendian
"XOR the low address bits" technique and a way of doing early detection of
possible floating-point exceptions. (They may have a patent on the microTLB
for instruction fetch as well. There are many others too.) Most of the core
RISC work was done at universities so the work was more openly
available. It was also slightly before the extreme emphasis on getting
patents in this area (both at universities and at companies).

Note that its basically impossible to build x86 clones without an Intel
cross licensing agreement in place. Which is why Cyrix/NexGen/etc. all had
to find fabs owned by companies with Intel patent cross licenses. I don't
expect the situation for IA64 to be any different.

Yes, DEC and Sun have both (recently) signed cross licensing agreements
with Intel. These agreements involved DEC and Sun pledging support for
IA64. So if Merced and followon kick butt, Sun and DEC will likely be
selling IA64 based hardware (in addition to SPARC and Alpha). If not
(i.e. Merced is a dog) lots of stuff will be up for grabs.

Finally, the details matter. A competitor to Merced has to be faster,
available in quantity in a timely manner, and priced right. It needs to
have widespread system vendor support so you can actually buy one in a
computer for a reasonable price. Then it needs to have applications
software or a very solid migration strategy and a compelling reason for
developers to support it in the future. Its not like you lay down a cool
new architecture on paper and then you're done. (Hey, Microunity had that
in spades :-)) To take the Alpha example again, its taken five years and
numerous attempts to get something as competitive (mostly pricewise) as the
current crop of 21164 and 21164PC based boxes out there and still the
market doesn't really care.

For comparison, how hard is it to write a Web browser? Why are Netscape and
Microsoft the only two anyone cares about?  What would you say to someone
starting up a company to build a new browser to compete against Navigator
and IE? Do you think this is the most profitable thing a talented software
team could be working on?

-Z-



