Ketil Z Malde <ketil@ii.uib.no> writes:
> Shouldn't a working compiler either:
> 
> 	compile the program to working object code for all cases of
> 	optimization and or debugging options
> or
> 	issue an error message to the effect that the program is illegal?
--

There are cases where this is impossible, or not defined in the
language. Consider the following:

foo() { int a; bar(&a);}

bar(int *x) {
	x[0] = 1;
	x[1] = 2;
}

You can generalize this example such that the precise information you
need from flow analysis is clearly not available. In this particular
example, the error will be shown depending on the stack layout of the
frame in procedure 'foo'. When not optimizing, all of the variables in
'foo' would be allocate to the stack, creating a large frame -- and
the code might not immediately die. With optimization, more variables
are promoted to registers, resulting in a shorter frame. It's more
likely that 'bar' will overwrite e.g.., the return stack information
in the frame, leading to an obvious error.

Some of these kinds of problems are related to weak type checking in
the C language. If someone was using Ada95 and told me they had errors
when they invoked the optimizer, I would be more suspicious of the
compiler. In C, I'm almost always suspicious of the program.


-- 
Dirk Grunwald                 Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder



