Dirk Grunwald <grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu> wrote in article
<qxpiusp3wnn.fsf@foobar.cs.colorado.edu>...
> "Ant Moore" <AntMoore@btinternet.com.nospam> writes:
> > Optimization doesn't have anything to do with "non-standard" coding
> > practices. If the code is legal (in that it compiles) then the only
> ---
> 
> This is the root of your problem. Just because code compiles doesn't
> mean it's 'legal'. Optimization can expose errors in your code due to
> code movement, use of temporaries, differences in the memory layout --
> any number of things. If your code is 'correct', as opposed to
> 'legal', these shouldn't matter.

I accept that the definition of "legal" == "it compiles" is not
particularly strong. But that is not the root of "my problem".

My point is that sometimes optimizers *do* get it wrong. But they
are not alone; even when not optimizing, compilers have been known to
have bugs too. Why should the optimizer be any less bug-prone?

Your point is valid too, of course. Before condemning the optimizer, 
it is worth investigating *why* the code has failed; it may indeed
be that the code is not "correct", even though "legal" Or it may
just be a compiler/optimizer fault, or a bug in a library... 

Cheers,

Ant. (Tamworth, England)

