Ant Moore wrote:
> 
> Optimization doesn't have anything to do with "non-standard" coding
> practices. If the code is legal (in that it compiles) then the only
> difference between optimized code and un-optimized code should be
> the execution speed (or size, or a combination of both, if that's what
> you're setting your optimization for). Granted, this can fall down if
> internal program timings are dependent on the execution time of a
> particular chunk of code, but then you are simply getting what you
> deserve for writing poor code.

"Internal program timing" isn't the only programming error that can 
come out of the woodwork when optimization is enabled. Another 
is that uninitialized variables "break" more often in optimized code.
I saw one of these a couple of weeks ago - the program worked great
until we used /OPTIMIZE; it turned out that it assumed an
uninitialized counter started out at zero.

Just because a problem happens (only) in optimized builds doesn't
mean there's a bug in the optimizer. It just means more investigation
is required. (And if there is a bug and you want it fixed, please report 
it along with a short reproducer program!)

