Arun Sharma <asharma@this.is.not.me.netscape.com> writes:

>Can Digital or Sun not build a next generation RISC processor with a
>Merced style (EPIC) instruction set ? A simple binary to binary
>translator might do compile time analysis of old binaries and store
>the explicit dependency information in the new instruction set
>binaries ?

>Is there anything wrong with this approach ?


Nothing stops them from doing it.  Maybe they are doing it now.  But unless
they feel they can do noticeably better than Merced either performance
wise (which will be completely unknown until it sees the light of day) or
cost wise (unlikely, with the volumes Intel will doing) it wouldn't be
worth it.  Because if they are going to change their instruction set, there
is no more penalty to go to Merced rather than something of their own design.
Their current designs still have some room for growth, so they will certainly
get an opportunity to see how well Merced does before they commit any real
cash to this, IMHO.

Sun is already going to be porting Solaris to EPIC (just like they have an
x86 version now, so this doesn't indicate any lack of belief in Sparc) so
they are in a pretty nice position.  If the Sparc starts lagging being Merced
in performance, they just have to start putting Merced chips in the boxes
they sell, and can slowly discontinue the Sparc line.  I.e., don't make any
new Sparcs, but sell the machines until you can buy Merced-based machines that
can run the translated code at pretty much the same speed or better.  Sort
of like Apple did with the 68K -> PowerPC migration.

-- 
