From: Jerome Fine <jhfine@idirect.com>
Subject: Zip Drives with RT-11 and Help for Write Protect
Date: 1997/12/15
Message-ID: <3495B6B6.ADA8DD5@idirect.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 298505883
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Newsgroups: alt.sys.pdp11,vmsnet.pdp-11,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.dec.micro,comp.os.vms


This information is directed at anyone who wishes to use
a removable media device of medium capacity (100 MBytes)
for transfer at hard disk drive speeds - well not the actual
media via snail mail, but the information after the cartridge
is inserted into the drive.

The information will probably also apply to Jazz drives by
Iomega, but I have not had the opportunity to test the hardware.

A Zip drive cartridge can have a file structure of RT-11 and
still be recognized on a PC under both MS-DOS mode and
Windows 95.  (I have not tested this, but I presume the same
thing would apply to RSTS/E and RSX-11).  On this hardware,
I used the host adapter supplied by Iomega that came with the
Zip SCSI insider (10714) disk drive.  Since the hardware
connection is SCSI, I was also able to use PUTR by John Wilson
to MOUNT the Zip cartridge and do normal PUTR commands
which would reference the files that came from an RT-11 system
on a PDP-11.  I don't want to make this note too long, so if
you are interested in any details that I have omitted, please e-mail.

On the PDP-11 side, I recently helped some friends move from
RD53s to SCSI hard drives and a Zip drive for removable media.
The two host adapters that were tried were from CMD - first a
CQD 200/M and next a CQD 220 A/TM.  Both were successful
in referencing the Zip drive (obviously the SCSI hard drive was
not in question).  There are some minor differences in booting
the system when a dual 11/73 is used and DEC boot ROMs
are the source of the boot code (between the two CQD boards).
However, once that hurdle is overcome, both of the CMD
host adapters functioned in a satisfactory manner.  I also think
that almost any host adapter would be able to handle the Zip
drive since it is just another SCSI-2 device.  I also had the same
two drives (SCSI hard drive and Zip drive) on the Iomaga
SCSI host adapter on the PC.

NotE that the capacity of the Zip cartridge is exactly 65536 * 3
blocks (3 partitions under RT-11).

One feature that is very functional is the ability to  "WRITE  PROTECT"
the Zip cartridge.  Even  "READ  PROTECT"  is available.  However,
this feature is done via software and  IS  AVAILABLE  ONLY  VIA
THE  PC at the present time (and probably a MAC).  The feature
functions properly.  It is just not available at present on an actual PDP-11
using a CMD host adapter.

I have tried to resolve this last aspect.  I do not consider this lack directly
on the PDP-11 to be a major problem due to the availability of a PC -
in most cases, must more available than the PDP-11.  While I have
contacted both Iomega and CMD about this aspect of not being able
to implement the  "WRITE  PROTECT"  feature under RT-11, there
has been neither an official response nor official interest, although
everyone a discussed the matter with at both companies were
extremely helpful and cordial.  First, we don't even know if the
MSCP protocol which is used under RT-11 to access the ZIP
drive and cartridge could handle that aspect.  Second, Iomega
may have to consider the details as a company trade secret.
In any case, if anyone out there might be able to shed some light
on how this might be implemented under the MSCP protocol
on RT-11, I would be willing to make the code changes to the
DU.MAC device driver source file.

In summary, Zip drives are able to serve as an exchange media
of medium size (100 MBytes) between a PC and a PDP-11
and have been tested with an RT-11 file structure.  They can
also be used to simply store data on a removable media for
the PDP-11 which is sufficient in size to be much more useful
that TK50 tapes and much, much faster.  The old drawback
on a PDP-11 might be the cost of a host adapter.  Old ones
are in short supply and new ones approach $ US 1500.

I hope the above will be of interest to RT-11 users in particular
and PDP-11 users in general.

If anyone wishes to accept the challenge of making the  "WRITE
PROTECT"  functions available under RT-11, that would be a
great bonus.  We can never underestimate whether some company
is interested enough in RT-11 to support this (for the RT-11
world) new and useful size in removable media.

Please contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
RT-11/TSX-PLUS  User/Addict





