In article <3495B6B6.ADA8DD5@idirect.com>,
Jerome Fine  <jhfine@idirect.com> wrote:
> First, we don't even know if the
>MSCP protocol which is used under RT-11 to access the ZIP
>drive and cartridge could handle that aspect.

It depends on what you call "the MSCP protocol".  Most SCSI host
adapters - CMD, Emulex, Dilog, etc. - allow arbitrary SCSI command
packets to be passed to devices from device drivers.  One of
the difficulties is that each manufacturer implements this
feature in a different way.  Usually this feature is undocumented
or available only using special drivers or sometimes only available
from "configuration" mode.

For those controllers which do allow arbitrary commands to be passed
by issuing special MSCP packets, the place you want to look in the
RT-11 docs is the Software Support Manual.  In my 5.5 doc set, it's
page 10-47 which documents .SPFUN 371.  You have to know a lot
more about MSCP than the RT-11 manuals tell you, but basically
you can build any MSCP command you feel like and give it to the
device.  I know that CMD has extensions to the MSCP protocol to
support arbitrary SCSI command packets via MSCP, as they have
special tools available for VMS to allow you to low-level-format
SCSI drives while the system is running.

>In any case, if anyone out there might be able to shed some light
>on how this might be implemented under the MSCP protocol
>on RT-11, I would be willing to make the code changes to the
>DU.MAC device driver source file.

Again, look in your documents - the RT-11 end of it is all there
for you to see.  There's no need to make any changes to DU.MAC;
just use the documented features.

I know that my RT-11 advice doesn't help you any in extracting the information
from IOMEGA about how they implement write-protect and password
protection.  If you've got the kilobucks, the definitive answer
is to buy a SCSI bus analyzer, or to press a logic analyzer into
service as one.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



