Tim Shoppa <shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca> wrote:
>It depends on what you call "the MSCP protocol".  Most SCSI host
>adapters - CMD, Emulex, Dilog, etc. - allow arbitrary SCSI command
>packets to be passed to devices from device drivers.

With the CMD controllers, I thought it was only the "/P" models that could
pass through raw SCSI commands?  I'd love to be wrong.

>I know that my RT-11 advice doesn't help you any in extracting the information
>from IOMEGA about how they implement write-protect and password
>protection.  If you've got the kilobucks, the definitive answer
>is to buy a SCSI bus analyzer, or to press a logic analyzer into
>service as one.

I've reverse engineered the Zip commands to set/reset the protection status,
haven't figured out how to detect the current status but hopefully it's not
that complicated.  They use opcodes defined as vendor-specific in the SCSI-2
spec so there's definitely no way to trick a controller that doesn't have a
pass-through command, into sending these commands as a result of normal MSCP
commands.

Zip-specific SCSI commands:

0C 00 00 00 NN 00	unprotect (from WP or NA state)
			NN is password length (up to 20h), 0 if none
			(No Access *requires* a password)
0C 02 00 00 00 00	write protect (no password)
0C 03 00 00 NN 00	write protect (with password)
0C 05 00 00 NN 00	read/write protect (always with password)
0C 0A 00 00 00 00	unprotect (from WP, no password) until eject
0C 0B 00 00 NN 00	unprotect (from WP w/password) until eject
0C 0D 00 00 NN 00	unprotect (from NA w/password) until eject

06 00 xx 00 NN 00	does something, maybe accesses private disk areas?
			I suspect this command is used to detect whether a
			disk is protected.
no idea what xx is (subfunction code?), NN seems to be length of data returned
(in bytes).

John Wilson
D Bit



