In article <01bd08a5$99c08900$930d0dc3@superbabe>, "ghandy" <ghandy007@hotmail.com> writes:
:I would like to sell a vax3100, but have a few questions before I do.

  If the front says "VAXstation 3100" on the medallion, it's likely either
  a VAXstation 3100 model 30 or a VAXstation 3100 model 40 -- the latter
  has the same electronics and a slightly taller box, differing only in
  the addition of some internal expansion bays.

  There are various "3100"-class systems, including the VAXstation 3100
  series, the MicroVAX 3100 series, and the VAXserver 3100 series.

  Rather than rummaging around inside the box, use the power-up self-tests
  to identify the system, and to determine how much (working) memory is in
  the system, and to determine what errors might be present.  (If the
  power-up tests cannot be run, the system is worth rather less.)

  Most MicroVAX and VAXstation CPUs will show a KA-code, such as KA42-B,
  when the CPU powers up.  This is the CPU type.  There are also usually
  commands to examine the memory -- on older VAXstation systems, the
  memory is displayed via a TEST 50 command.  On more recent systems,
  there are often a variety of SHOW commands and online HELP at the >>>
  prompt.  By posting this code, we can identify the particular CPU used.

  On the VAXstation 3100, the "MEM" line shown by >>> TEST 50 looks like
  "MEM mmmm.ssss", where mmmm is the number of megabytes of working memory,
  and ssss is usually 0001, which is the (good) status code value.

:3) What value do you think this stuff has, these days?

  Anywhere from helping defray the costs of shipping the system to the
  dump, to -- for the recent members of the MicroVAX 3100 series -- fast,
  and moderately expensive.  Given that I expect this is a VAXstation
  3100 series system, and probably a low-end one at that, I'd tend to
  expect the "defray shipping" end of the price scale.

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
