In article <34960cf7.0@news.wizvax.net>, John Wilson <wilson@dbit.com> wrote:
>In article <674po3$nf8$1@nntp.ucs.ubc.ca>,
>Tim Shoppa <shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca> wrote:
>>It depends on what you call "the MSCP protocol".  Most SCSI host
>>adapters - CMD, Emulex, Dilog, etc. - allow arbitrary SCSI command
>>packets to be passed to devices from device drivers.
>
>With the CMD controllers, I thought it was only the "/P" models that could
>pass through raw SCSI commands?  I'd love to be wrong.

AFAIK, all CMD controllers offer some way of passing through raw SCSI
commands, though with some the only way I know how to do it is to drop
into "configuration" mode, which effectively takes the controller
off-line to any OS that might be running.  With some OS's - like RT11 -
you can gracefully recover from a MSCP controller that's gone offline.
Other OS's are very ungraceful about this condition.

In any event, I agree with your point John - that any such pass-through
system is going to be vendor specific, and in some cases model-specific
or firmware-specific.  It'd be a nightmare to support or maintain!

Getting back to Jerry's original idea, I don't like the concept of
software-controlled write protection.  I want a button on the front
of the drive - or a tab on the drive cartridge - that can be physically
set!

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



