Jeffrey Potoff <jpotoff@atlas.cheme.cornell.edu> wrote in article
<34955C3B.167E@atlas.cheme.cornell.edu>...

> Maybe C is different, but in all cases with Fortran I've found that
> 'optimization errors' were due to non-standard coding practices.  If
> your code is truely portable you should be able to compile it with
> maximum optimization on any platform.  Besides, if you can't take 
> advantage of the compiler optimizations what's the point of running
> on faster hardware ?  You're essentially throwing away CPU cycles 
> (and money) because your code isn't optimized.  If someone offered you
> a new machine that was twice as fast as your old one at no additional
> cost you'd take it, wouldn't you ?  
> 
> Jeff
> 

Optimization doesn't have anything to do with "non-standard" coding
practices. If the code is legal (in that it compiles) then the only
difference between optimized code and un-optimized code should be 
the execution speed (or size, or a combination of both, if that's what
you're setting your optimization for). Granted, this can fall down if
internal program timings are dependent on the execution time of a 
particular chunk of code, but then you are simply getting what you
deserve for writing poor code.

Taking your machine analogy, yes I would have the twice-as-fast machine
for nothing, unless that machine didn't work properly, or crashed
sometimes, or didn't give the right answers, (or worse still *sometimes*
didn't give the right answers)... then I'd stick with the slow machine
that worked properly.

 
Cheers,

Ant. (Tamworth, England)

