Subject: Re: Configuration of KRQ-50
Date: 1997/12/15
Message-ID: <67254m$g7r$1@sunburst.ccs.yorku.ca>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 298243827
References: <349080AF.32D0@pol88a.polito.it>
Organization: Dept. of Computer Science, York University
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.dec.micro,comp.os.vms,alt.sys.pdp11


Tidbits from the KRQ50-SF Installation Guide that came (fortunately)
with the M7552 and RRD40 I picked up at a surplus store recently.
(No, they don't have any more of them.)

Recommended module placement: at the tail end of the Qbus, but
ahead of any TKQ50s or RQDX3s.

Switch S1 (bank of ten dip switches) and jumper J7 (next to S1)
set the CSR address.  To quote from the manual:

  1.  Jumper J7 is the highest selectable address bit.  It is address
  bit 12.
  2.  Switch 10, the closest switch to J7 on the switchpack, is the
  second highest selectable address bit.  It is address bit 11.
  3.  Switch 1, the farthest switch from J7, is the lowest selectable
  address bit.  It is address bit 2.

  The preferred address for the KRQ50 is 17772150.  If this address
  is occupied by another MSCP disk controller (such as the M7555
  RQDX3 controller), the KRQ50 is set to the first available address
  starting at 17760334.

A bit is 1 if the corresponding switch is on, 0 if off.  J7 represents
a 1 when installed vertically (perpendicular to the backplane), 0 when
horizontal (parallel to the backplane).

For example, address 17760334 (which happens to be the factory
setting) is
	J7 horizontal (0)
	Switches 10-1 set to
	off off off off on on off on on on


The M7552 has a built-in power-up test; the results show up
in the two red LEDs on the top edge of the card (or on the
fancy bulkhead handle for a BA200 enclosure).  When the board
first receives power, both LEDs turn on; then LED 1 turns off;
then LED 2 also turns off.  Both LEDs should have turned off
within 10 seconds.  If LED 1 won't go off, the controller is
broken; if LED 2 won't go off, it might be the I/O cable (so
unplug the cable from the Berg connector on the M7552 and
try again).  The manual says you should unplug the cable to
the CD-ROM drive before testing; presumably this is just because
a defective drive may cause further confusion.  Mine works fine
with the drive connected.

Once the KRQ50 has been initialized by the OS, the LEDs will
blink in a regular counting pattern.  This doesn't mean the
diagnostics have failed.

The manual doesn't have much more to say than this, but I'll
be glad to look up answers to further questions.

I can't offer any insight on the RRD50's switches; I have an
RRD40.

Norman Wilson
norman@cs.yorku.ca



