>
>>[...]
>> Hereabouts, fewer and fewer new machines are being purchased from
>> Digital and more from competitors like SGI, HP, and Sun (most of the
>> recent action in workstation purchases in the last year or two has been
>> SGIs).
>>[...]
>
>SGI. Interesting choice. It might be just be the one UNIX
>workstation provider that's had more problems than Digital
>in the last couple of years. Of course they did announce
>a couple months back that they'll be offering Intel/NT systems....

I was really just reporting what seems to be happening rather than
trying to put any particular spin upon it. A lot depends upon the purpose
for which one is buying the machine. But, since it will obviously be
perceived that I am being biased anyway, SGI has some strong points:

 1.) A rather good Varsity Program for Universities to buy much of the
     SGI software pretty inexpensively (similar to CSLG).

 2.) A rather simple interface and rather easy system administration 
     for the non Unix saavy user (seemingly fewer calls from newbie SGI
     purchasers than most other popular Unix vendor box purchasers). Of
     course, when things go wrong (like a disk crash), they can require
     a lot more consultation.

 3.) A rather good reputation and good sales representation on campus.
     (DEC's sales presence on campus seems to be almost non-existent these
     days; I couldn't begin to name who is supposed to be our current
     campus sales rep. at the moment). At a time when most of the major
     vendors seem to be consolidating sales forces and withdrawing from
     direct day-to-day campus availability, SGI actually seems to be
     making efforts to maintain an active local presence (not currently
     true of Sun or DEC to my knowledge). Our Sun sales rep. for OSU is
     based in Indianapolis, I dunno *where* our DEC sales rep is based.
     I think HP still has a local presence.

 4.) Hardware phase-outs (due to OS support dropping) have been rather
     gradual processes thus far (no DECStation/Ultrix to Alpha/OSF1
     type transitions).

Agreed, I think the announcement of SGI Intel/NT systems may be a
mistake (but that remains to be seen). From an income perspective
(despite stock market hoopla of last several quarters), SGI seems to be
trying to diversify itself at a time when DEC is consolidating.
SGI/MIPS sells a *lot* of chips for use beyond the workstation market.
I perceive that most of SGI's problems relate to their continuing
effort to digest their Cray acquisition and to make it profitable.
And I will certainly agree that SGI at the corporate level can be a
hard company with which to deal from time to time. Still, they seem
to be making an effort to make inroads in the higher education market
locally (with DEC and Sun, the kindest that can be said is that their
efforts seem to be somewhat "haphazard" in the last few years).

