In article <349502D1.4DB83CEB@uvo.dec.com>, John McNulty <jm@uvo.dec.com> writes:
>mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu wrote:
>> 
>> So, vote with your feet and your wallet, dump Digital, avoid Exchange, and
>> install PMDF from Innosoft. 

<SNIP>
>
>Nah, don't do that.  Install Digital UNIX instead and set yourself
>up with an LDAP + IMAP mail solution.  You can get both from the
>Internet Alphaserver kit, or install one of Netscapes server products.
>That way you get a solution that fits, PC, UNIX, Mac, etc clients. 

You imply that PMDF cannot adequately service ("fit") the named client
platforms. If you actually have some evidence to support your assertion,
please present it. 

PMDF is an enterprise grade mail system, so of course PMDF supports the
major email standards allowing it to "fit" any email client that uses those 
standards.  In addition to the more familiar (to us) POP,IMAP, X.500 and
LDAP functions, it also has specialized gateways to other mail systems such
as PROFS, cc:MAIL, Novell Groupwise, and a slew of others.  Perhaps
Netscape's mail server can handle these as well, but for sure just adding
an IMAP server to DU does not provide this sort of functionality, and a DU
machine configured in that manner (with nothing else added) is in no way
adequate for providing enterprise wide email service, except perhaps for
that rare company that does not have any of these other email systems. 

From http://www.infoworld.com/pageone/testcenter/pc030496.htm

  "There may be no absolutely perfect E-mail backbone in the world, but PMDF
   has come closer to ideal than any we've seen so far."

