In article <881916965.181087@newton.dialix.com.au>, henryt@perth.dialix.com.au (Henry Titchen) writes:
:I have found an old computer a vax 3400.    Is there anywhere on the net
:where I can learn more about this computer?

  Start with the pointers present in the OpenVMS Frequently Asked Questions.

    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/vms

  You likely have found a KA640 CPU Q-bus MicroVAX 3400 system, with imbedded
  DSSI and Ethernet controllers, a console serial line connection, and (if
  memory serves :-) four MB on the CPU module.  KA650 memory was used to
  expand the available memory.

  The CPU used on this system was the second-generation microprocessor VAX,
  the CVAX chip.  This system was roughly 2.4 times the performance of the
  original VAX, the VAX-11/780.  Current NVAX-based microprocessors are
  roughly 32.0 (or better) times the performance of the VAX-11/780.

  I think the KA640 bulkhead used the MMJ connection for the console serial
  line connection -- a small modular connector, with the locking tab offset
  to the side from the tab used on US telephones, to prevent 90V ring signals
  from frying UARTs.  (See the OpenVMS FAQ for the MMJ pinout and for the
  adapter part numbers.)

  This system is supported by the OpenVMS hobbyist license available via US
  DECUS, see http://www.montagar.com.  (I do not know if this DECUS program
  is available in your particular region, contact your local DECUS folks for
  further assistance and questions.)

  The minimum OpenVMS (VMS) version for this system is V5.0-2A.  The current
  OpenVMS release is V7.1.

