clock speed has little to do with the performance of a chip. end of argument.


>
>I don't think memory is the issue. We are theorizing that something
>isn't configured properly in the Alpha, or Digital UNIX 4/3.2. Or, is it
>just not a responsive OS? Something to fuel discussion....

Heres something to ask yourself:
Since youre using DUNIX, you probably have the DEC compiler.
What compiler are you running on the Sun? 
You are essentially telling us that two separate programs, designed 
to more or less do roughly the same kind of work, are perfroming 
differently, and then implying that the mahcine or OS theyre running under
are the culpritsd for the disparity! 
Look at the obvious thing: youre running two separate programs!
If you really want to measure performance, run the SAME program on each 
machine. 
In fact, the DEC compiler does fancy things and this takes a lot of time. 
It actually does a lot more work than gcc or your compiler in Solaris
is probably doing. This takes time. While it doesent rule out machine
or OS configuration possibilities, im mentioning that you cant
expect a good performance measure by running two different porgrams
on two different machines. 
Try taking a program for which you have source, compiling it
on both machinesa, and measuring performance then.
Things liek compilers have a ton of machine-specific stuff in them, since
the optimization is 9/10 of the battle, and Alpha is a machine which is
very sensitive to poor optimization on the part of a compiler. The DEC
compiler does incredible optimization but it has to do a lot of black magic
with your code before it gets there, and that takes a lot of time. 

just my two bits..

isildur




