In article <34891ECB.222E@videotron.ca>, jf mezei <"[non-spam]jfmezei"@videotron.ca> says:
>
>re: assembler code generated by compilers on Intel and Alpha with 
>    Alpha being faster.
>
>Yeah, so Alpha is faster. That is not new.
>
>But who cares ?
>
>Company X has a package. They compile it on various UNIX, on WIN32 for
>intel, perhaps for the MAC and if lucky, on NT for Alpha.  These are the
>popular packages for today.
>
>And these companies will use tools that can cross-compile source code
>that shares a LOT between platforms. Programmer efficiency is more
>important than instruction pipelining efficiency on a single marginal
>platform which ALPHA is.
>
>So, packages are written for the MAC or WINDOWS, and then just ported to
>ALPHA-NT or UNIX. Those ports don't spend much time or resources to
>really maximize performance.

So? If the Alpha compiler is any good, it'll generate code at least
as well-matched to an Alpha as the I386 compiler does to a Pentium.

By the way, Spec benchmarks include the compiler performance (at least
wrt the benchmark programs). Were the ones posted earlier for an Alpha
running NT? If so, then the comparison is direct. (If they were for
an Alpha running VMS or Digital Unix, then the NT figure may differ,
but this shouldn't be by a lot)

If you aren't number-crunching or games-playing (same thing really!)
it's easy to over-rate processor performance compared to other critical
areas like hard disk performance or network bandwidth. For many classes
of problem, a slower processor with an extra (32? 64? 128? ...) Mb
or RAM will outperform compared to spending the extra on a faster
processor.



