Bob, and everyone else who's replied to me in here, or written to
me offline ... I hear what you're saying, and I do understand and
empathize.  You're not really telling me something I didn't already
know, but it doesn't hurt to hear it again.

As for my "brainwashing", well, someone has to stand up, be counted
and wave the DEC flag every once in a while.  So don't blame me for
trying.

What I would like to say to you all though is this.  If you have a
bad experience working with Digital, don't sit on it, beef to your
colleagues, friends, family, or blow off steam in comp.sys.dec.  Take
it up directly with Digital.  To the best of my knowledge, every 
customer facing organization within Digital has a customer complaints
procedure, so take the time to find out who your local account manager
or Digital Representative is, and put your thoughts in writing.  If you
can't find such a person, get the name of building manager of your 
nearest DEC office and write to him/her.  Explain what your complaint
is, give details about why your expectations weren't met, and if
possible suggest what should have happened, or where you would like
to see improvements.  I know it's hard sometimes, but try and be 
constructive.  

If you're not satisfied with the response, don't leave it there. Go
higher!  Heck, write to Bob Palmer himself if necessary.  As customers,
you have a voice ... so make yourself heard.  

Digital won't fix its image problems all by itself, it needs direct
feedback, both good and bad.  But take a more direct approach,
comp.sys.dec is the wrong tool to be doing this.  I have no idea who
else in Digital reads this newsgroup, but I'd be very surprised if
there were any managers who did, and it's the managers you need to
get to.  So use the channels and procedures inside DEC that are put
in place to help you.  

Finally, thanks for all the feedback I've had, both good (yes I have
had some pleasant comments ;-)  and bad.


John
