Ummm, the issue of marketing is something DEC should know how to do
(better than any of us out here in the field). The reflection that they
seem to be way off course lately is an indication of serious problems.

I do second-level support for Unix platforms for a major University
(check the posting headers to solve the mystery :-).  Over the past
several years (hereabouts starting mainly with the DECStation OSF/1
debacle), from my vantage point, I've seen DEC slide more and more into
obscurity here on campus. Apparently, the thinking behind the "no OSF/1
ports to the DECStations" for marketing purposes may have been that it
would force folks to buy Alphas (having a series of on again, off again
announcements was really poor too). What it *really* accomplished from
my perspective was:

1.) It *really* cheesed off folks who had purchased DECStations (some
    very recently, some who had believed that OSF/1 DECStation support
    stammering). Many of these folks then became subscribers to the 
    doctrine "Fool me once shame on you. Fool me twice hame on me".

2.) It really made the folks who had recommended purchasing DECStations
    like the new 5000/260 look bad (folks in support and management
    positions). In the longer run, this may have hurt worse than point 1.

3.) It (and this was probably the most tragic aspect of it) caused
    folks to reevalute vendors. After all, if they had to buy new
    hardware *and* change OSes anyway to move into the future, why not
    look at the competition.  I would think that in marketing one thing
    you would *never* want to do with the legacy customer base is to
    make the alternative of a "clean break" look to be on par with the
    transition to a new line of equipment by your current vendor (DEC
    in this case). Some will choose to forge ahead with the same
    vendor, but a lot of them (especially those still stinging from
    points 1 & 2) will figure that if they have to start over, it might
    as well be with another vendor altogether.

Hereabouts, fewer and fewer new machines are being purchased from
Digital and more from competitors like SGI, HP, and Sun (most of the
recent action in workstation purchases in the last year or two has been
SGIs).  I've also seen some erosion to PeeCee based NT boxen (though
for serious server work, Unix still seems to rule here -- though MS
regularly pressures the upper eschelon folks to go "all MS" and they
mean all PeeCee MS, I have never heard them bother to mention Alpha NT
alternative).  DEC seems to keep changing strategies and sometimes it
seems that each new plan is worse than the last. I can recall a number
of years back when several DEC thralls declared that Alpha was the
"make or break" future of the company. Then lately it comes to light
that they seemingly never got close to production capacity for the FAB
plant.  Then, DEC cheesed and probably alienated probably the most
loyal portion remaining of their legacy customer base (VMS fans) by
declaring that Digital Unix would be ported to Merced, but there were
no current plans to do so for OpenVMS (which implied, to the untrained
eye anyway, that Alpha's future is no longer certain and shiney and
that OpenVMS is eventually headed for bit bucket land).  I'm really
uncertain what the thinking is with this strategy.  You can review a
whole series of bad marketing moves (or just plain bad corporate
decisions) over the past several years (and people here have).

But, from our perspective in the field, it is not up to us to save DEC. 
It is up to DEC to save DEC. From my perspective, hey, I support
Unixen other than Digital Unix. Sure, it will be sad if DEC and D.U.
go under, but I've already seen a shift to the other Unix platforms I
support which more than makes up for the erosion in DEC sales here on
campus. I can adapt (and have quite a while back) and support other
Unixes.  It really isn't a great loss to me or my livelihood, but it is
a *huge* loss to DEC.

Quite frankly, having Alpha fail (or at least not succeed like it should
have) should be perceived as a failure of DEC management and marketing
of epic proportions. DEC had a large technological lead at Alpha intro. 
Opportunities were missed, the competitors caught up, and now DEC seems
to be angling to make it into the next millenium any way they can
(apparently by attempting to cling to MS (oh so fragile) coattails, as
the official NT support organization, near as I can figure).

