Although it may not feel like it to the recipient, and it doesn't always 
offer the solution to the problem discussed (ie, may not seem 
"constructive"), criticism is still valuable.  Criticism from your
customers is even more valuable, because if you ignore it, they will go
elsewhere.  If we didn't care, we wouldn't even bother to criticize, right?
The problem is, Digital as a corporate entity doesn't seem to care that we
care, and there is precious little evidence to suggest that they even hear
us. 

>
>If you're not satisfied with the response, don't leave it there. Go
>higher!  Heck, write to Bob Palmer himself if necessary.  As customers,
>you have a voice ... so make yourself heard.  

My two cents on this, regarding contacting Digital.  I had been trying
without success for some time to get them to pick up the raw data from the
PCVMS poll (you'd think they'd want it, right?  Couple of hundred people
willing to plunk down their money for a certain product - if Digital ever
builds it - summing into the millions of dollars.)  After some prodding
from Ken Fairfield and others, I finally sent letters (not email, real
letters) to everybody I could think of in the upper echelons.  The letter
sent to Wes Melling did result in a retrieval of the raw data and a brief
email note from one of his lieutenants.  At this point, there's nothing
more that I can report to you on the PCVMS front.  However, without going
into detail, let me just say that I am now reasonably certain that many,
perhaps all of the people, in the upper realms of Digital management do
NOT follow these newsgroups regularly.  The most likely scenario is that
they never look at them at all.  So it it is probably pointless to post
anything here if you expect it to be read by anybody at Digital who is high
enough up to act on it.  

From one data point, it does seem that they do read at least some of their
regular postal mail though.  So, in terms of getting the message across, I
think that the most effective method would be something like an opinion
poll verifying what we've been saying, followed immediately by a flood of
letters directed at Digital management pointing to those results.  Example,
we've all been ragging on Digital for being totally absent, or negatively 
viewed, in the public's perception.  Imagine that some site runs a fair
poll asking the appropriate questions, such as 

  score (company) for innovation
  score (company) for engineering competence
  score (company) for customer relations
  score (company) for "I can count on them to provide what I want"
  score (company) for likelihood long term survival
  score (company) for your willingness to buy their equipment
  score (company) for your management's willingness to buy their equipment
  score (company) for your familiarity with their equipment
  score (company) for your management's familiarity with their equipment
  score (company) for management
  score (company) for price competitiveness in (0-$2000,$2000-$4000, etc.)
  score (company) for service
  etc.

etc., where (company) is repeated for each of IBM, HP, SGI, Sun, and
Digital.  Such a poll should exclude employees of all companies listed,
probably by requiring email addresses and some sort of answerback
confirmation by email, and chopping all of the sun.com, digital.com, etc.
addresses.  After the poll has run to a large enough number of respondants,
trigger a letter writing campaign, which just says: GO PICK UP THE NUMBERS.
This is an odd form of advocacy, but we want them to know how they really
do stack up against their competitors in the eyes of the end users. 

(I'm not volunteering for this task though, PC VMS has been more than
enough of an investment in advocacy for me.) 

