Jerome Fine replies (with his one-tenth of a cent):

Let me first say that I am and old PDP-11 hacker ("thrake" I think is the
term - don't know what it means or where it came from).  I have used
VMS as a user on a number of occasions, in addition to RSTS/E and
RSX-11.  But my real joy and the challenge is the RT-11 operating
system.  Back in 1970, some hardware designers did one really good
job at a superb hardware architecture which was eventually extended
to the 22 bit Qbus and from a physical address space of 64 KBytes
to 4 MBytes.  What with overlays and a virtual disk (VM:), the
hardware eventually became (by about 1985) probably one of the
better computer systems at that time for the target applications.

In addition, the operating system in RT-11 and probably the others
as well, were receiving excellant attention by way of design concepts
and eventually many of these were implemented in a very reasonable
manner.  I venture to say that most of the hobby users today would
not still be having fun with RT-11 if it were not such a pleasant system
to work with and on and inside of.

But, there is where the problem might be said to start.  Having done
such a termendous job on the two critical elements - CPU architecture
and operating system concepts - the users perhaps expected too much
in the way of marketing and implementation.

I used to always say I have a very, very dry joke to tell about DEC:
"What is DECs marketing policy?"  Answer:  "Add nothing!"
"What is DECs hardware policy?"  Answer:  "Add nothing!"
Both are said more in sorrow than in anger, but:  Example:
DEC buys a Micropolis 1325 for about $ 500;  they put a
zero on the end - add nothing; then they sell the RD53 for $ 5000
Very good marketing?  Now these may not be the exact numbers,
but they are certainly not far off.  And since DEC did that kind of
pricing consistantly (remember the cost of a box of 10 RX50s),
a 1000% markup was probably considered normal. Next example:
Hardware fellows take the Micropolis 1325 and add a zero ohm
resistor - yes they added nothing again - just a jumper of course
(I actually have seen this done first hand - not hearsay) to enable
the DEC controller (RQDX2) recognize the drive as an RD53.

Now other companies do virtually the same thing (Honeywell
with a thermostat I once saw and Seagate with some of their
line of disk drives I was one told) - maybe not so blantently
as 1000%, but sort of the same principle.  The real damage
comes with the kind of attitude that almost every DEC and
in some cases ex-DEC employees have toward customers.
Having been guided by Ken Olsen and upper management
in the 1980s with the add nothing markups and gained the
market dominance that could result in the BIbus disaster
(just my opinion - but proprietarty - my Netscape spelling
checker is on the fritz again - anyone out there who can tell
me why it works only at random), it seemed that DEC chose
to adopt a management style that filtered out or at least delayed
good technical ideas from being implemented and supported
and allowed bad ideas to become dominate.

And in RT-11, there seems to be the impression that this is
still the way to go.  Now let me be the first to say that Mentec
seems to have the responsibility for all PDP-11 software.
But if DEC can grant a hobby license for VMS users, why
not at least continue the policy for RT-11 enthusiasts.  It
isn't as if it was never done - Australia and New Zealand
had some sort of arrangement which I am led to believe
is no longer available.  And, moreover, Mentec seems to
believe that existing users of RT-11 are required to purchase
all Year 2000 bug fixes from Mentec - or else the altered
versions of the operating system are not supported.  Well of
course they are not supported.  Most of us don't even want
support.  We would just rather Mentec and DEC abandoned
RT-11 and let us fix it ourselves.  As it seems that a few have
already done.  After all, if you fellows in VMS had waited
for more than 5 years for another release from DEC
(version 5.6 of RT-11 release date around August 1992),
what would you begin to think?  But no, Mentec keeps
saying it will be done - and after more than two years of
the same song - it gets a little bit tiresome - especially after
they receive an offer to have it done without charge up
front - NO  FRONT  END  LOAD  PLAN.  But always
the same answer - one word - two letters - first letter "N".

So, in essence, I agree with Bob.  And although the above thoughts
are only my own opinions, I suspect that many others share them.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
RT-11/TSX-PLUS  User/Addict
Year 2000 Solutions for Legacy RT-11 Applications
(Sources not always required)





