In article <66sbh4$nk737@valley.rtpnc.epa.gov>, 
    	kang.sang@epamail.epa.gov (Sang Kang) writes:
> I'm using Quantum IV DLT tapes for backing up a database.
> With hardware compression. I'm getting on average 130 Gigabyte per tape.
> According to the manufacturer of both Quantum and DEC, I shouldn't get no
> more than 90gig per tape.
> Has anyone gotten same capacity?

        I _thought_ the TZ88's did 20/40 GB,  no?  And the just released
    TZ89 does 35/70 GB or so?

        Anyway, we have TZ87's and  TZ88's  (the latter run in TZ87 mode
    for  read-anywhere compatibility) that are supposed to do  10/20 GB.
    Without really paying close attention, we found we had written 33 GB
    during one of our (VMS) backups.  (The amount of  data  written  was
    determined by doing a DIRECTORY/SIZE on the tape and multiplying the
    number  tape  blocks written by the known (specified) size of a tape
    block, so I have good confidence in that number.)

        Now, the real key  here  is  that  the  compression one of these
    drives (or similarly compressing DAT or 8mm drives) depends strongly
    upon  the _data_ being written.  A factor-of-2 compression ratio  is
    just a guess, probably a conservative one at that, proffered by  the
    manufacturer.   Experience at SLAC several years back when IBM first
    supplied compressing versions of  their  cartridge tape drives (3480
    cartridges?   I  just  can't remember now...) was  that  compression
    ratios as high as 4:1 could be achieved e.g. on text files, but that
    physics data would only compress at 1.2:1 to 1.6:1.  The reason  was
    that physicists already compress  the  data  before writing it, that
    is, remove redundancies and use the smallest binary format possible,
    where  possible.   There's  not  much  left  for  the  tape  drive's
    compression algorithm to work with (think of trying  to  zip  a  zip
    file).
    
        So...130 GB on a 90 GB tape,  yeah,  I'd say it's possible, even
    likely  depending upon the data you're writing.  I wouldn't count on
    always getting that good compression.  If you need to know, you need
    to do a series of experiments and empirically determine the  _range_
    of compressions that your data will give.

        -Ken
