I need to replace our old Alphaserver 2100 4/233 (2 processors) early next 
year, so I've been doing a ton of research on what machine we should get next 
year. Our DEC supplier immediately came back with suggestions, but I did some 
research on my own.

We're going to be using this machine with NT and SQL Server. I wanted a 
benchmark to compare machines running this setup and I found the TPC set of 
benchmarks which tell both raw speed (transactions per minute or tpm) and 
bang-for-the-buck, or $/tpm. The list I found of $/tpm doesn't even have DEC in 
the top ten -- it's mostly Unisys and their Aquanta series of servers. So, I'm 
leaning toward Unisys at this point.

However, I have this sneaking suspicion that tpm may not be the correct way of 
comparing machines that do SQL. For instance, we have a 50-user environment, 
and when I'm watching the performance stats, tpm never gets up very high. On 
the other hand, we have a lot of people complaining about slow speed in some 
parts of our application (IMIS). I've indexed all the obvious fields, and now 
it's just looking like our Alphaserver 2100 is just generally slow. There are 
no tpm stats for it, but from what I can figure out, it can run at about 3100 
tpm. The Unisys machines are running at around 31,000 tpm, with $/tpm about 
$37. DEC's $/tpm are all up around $60 and up.

Someone convince me to stay with DEC!!!

Thanks,
Todd Wallace




