DEC, like IBM, has not learned that in the whole big world, 
to succeed big (i.e. widespread adoption) you have to offer 
what the BUYER wants to buy, rather than what YOU want to sell.
DEC has, since the demise of OEM sales of PDP-11s, tried
to make a "positioned" group of computers such that the lower
priced ones are crippled in some trivial way. In the old days
of the PDP-11 there were numerous OEMs that bought PDP11s,
either as whole board sets, single boards, or in the end
just chips, and packaged them in ways that did not have these
cripplings. This is similar to Apple and Power Computing.
DEC never offered in any form cheap VAXs that were not crippled.
They never sold VAX chips to OEMS. Its not clear to me what they are
doing with Alpha. 

To succeed in the large world these days the top of the line
chips have to be available in uncrippled systems at the
lowest possible price. I do not mean that low cost
memory type systems are crippled. Crippled, for example,
would be with something that limited memory to, oh say
32 megs. Or some sort of on-chip crippling that made the
top of the line chips run only using expensive proprietary
OSs. DEC actually apparently does this latter.

To recap, to succeed big, you have to have a whole line
of stuff that does not suffer from the "positioning" problem.
PCs are not like this: there is an obvious "marketing
position" for various type computers, but any computer
can in fact be used for any purpose, independent of the
seller's positioning ideas. People all the time use
"server positioned" computers for games and "el-cheapo
home systems" as servers, if they feel that's what they need.
And, may we note, that absolutely nothing eats CPU power
like games except scientific computing and some sorts of
graphics applications. But it is currently getting to the
point where the games and the scientists (including
engineering simulations) will be the only real top end of
any market size (this leaves out people like the NSA).
(Transaction processing is an entirely different beast
that's not so much a CPU power eater as an IO problem.)


Doug McDonald



