In article <66d5qn$aee@usenet.srv.cis.pitt.edu>, geest3+@pitt.edu (Gregg E Economou) writes:
:Some VAXen will halt the system and drop to the console monitor when 
:the console line recieves a BREAK character. This might be your problem.
:
:Christian J. Bauer <christian.bauer@passau.netsurf.de> wrote:
:>the VAX seem to spontaneaous reboot. I could remember that this was a
:>problem in the past under certain circumstances.
:>
:>Unfortunately, no Dump or error log files are generated. When coming
:>back to the console after a while (OPA0: is normally logged out), just
:>the boot prompt ">>>" appears after pressing enter .
:
:give us more informatio- for example, what are you using as console?
:a terminal? a PC? a line on a terminal server? a printer? a modem? 
:All of those and whatever else you could imaginably connect coudl all 
:be causing potential problems. 
..
:With what you gave us, my initial suggestion, that a break character is 
:being sent down the line by your console device, is probably 
:what is happening. Try just resuming the system's execution and seeing if 
:it goes back to normal. (im not fluent in vax 4000 console lingo, im a
:microVAX kind'a guy, so i dont knwo what the command would be.  ;)

  Is the console terminal being logged out and then turned off?  If so,
  that is probably the cause of the problem -- turning off the terminal
  on the console line is verboten: a number of serial terminals will
  generate serial line framing errors when power-cycled, and a framing
  error is another name for a <break> signal. 

  A "C" continue command might work if the system is standalone, but it
  probably won't work if the system is in a VMScluster.  (This assumes
  that you do not catch the halt right away, and immediately enter the
  continue command.)

