Todd Wallace wrote:
> 
> I need to replace our old Alphaserver 2100 4/233 (2 processors) early next
> year, so I've been doing a ton of research on what machine we should get next
> year. Our DEC supplier immediately came back with suggestions, but I did some
> research on my own.
> 
> We're going to be using this machine with NT and SQL Server. I wanted a
> benchmark to compare machines running this setup and I found the TPC set of
> benchmarks which tell both raw speed (transactions per minute or tpm) and
> bang-for-the-buck, or $/tpm. The list I found of $/tpm doesn't even have DEC in
> the top ten -- it's mostly Unisys and their Aquanta series of servers. So, I'm
> leaning toward Unisys at this point.

A quick glance over the TPC-C figures from www.tpc.org shows that the
DEC benchmark results are missing the most likely candidates for replacing
your 2100, ie the 1200 and the 800.

> 
> However, I have this sneaking suspicion that tpm may not be the correct way of
> comparing machines that do SQL.

The benchmarks are usually much simpler than most real-life applications.
"doing SQL" covers a multitude of sins.
 
>                                  For instance, we have a 50-user environment,
> and when I'm watching the performance stats, tpm never gets up very high. On
> the other hand, we have a lot of people complaining about slow speed in some
> parts of our application (IMIS). I've indexed all the obvious fields, and now
> it's just looking like our Alphaserver 2100 is just generally slow. 

In any computer system there is a bounding resource; the secret to improving
performance (assuming you've done everything you can to improve the application)
is to identify the bounding resource and improve _it_.

What's the average CPU utilization ?  If it is high (>70%) then a faster
CPU will probably improve performance.  What are the disks doing ?  If
I/O is concentrated on a small number of disks, then spreading the I/O
load among more disks may improve performance; using RAID 5 may be a 
simple way of doing this.  Changing 5400RPM disks for 7200 RPM disks may 
also improve performance.  I don't know if you can measure I/O throughput, 
but spreading I/O over extra SCSI buses may be worthwhile if the bus
is saturated.

>                                                                      There are
> no tpm stats for it, but from what I can figure out, it can run at about 3100
> tpm. The Unisys machines are running at around 31,000 tpm, with $/tpm about
> $37. DEC's $/tpm are all up around $60 and up.

The Unisys systems on the TPC spreadsheet are all multiprocessor Pentium Pro
systems.  The tpm figures are all between 2000 and 13000, for a $/tpm of
$39 to $43.  The CPUs are roughly the same speed as the Alpha 4/233 (on SpecFP
benchmarks at least).  Where are you getting your benchmark results ?

> Someone convince me to stay with DEC!!!

There's more to choosing a system vendor than benchmark performance.  $/tpm
figures are a _very_ poor indicator - you really need to compare actual 
costs of your target systems; hardware, software, maintenance.

Go with whichever vendor you want, but don't do it on the strength of a 
benchmark !

