In article <881668802.521884@pong.ti.telenor.net>,
Tor Houghton  <th@omaha.nextel.no> wrote:
>Tried reading the SWXCRMON and SWXCRMGR floppy on a SUN machine the other
>day, Digital claiming them to be UFS. The Sun machine failed to mount
>them. Then I tried a Linux machine, which, although mounted them, could
>not find any files on the floppy.

This is hardly surprising.  UFS is does not use network byte order so
of course you can't read UFS filesystems from a DEC machine (which is
litt-endian) on a SUN machine (which is big-endian).

As for the problems with Linux -- who knows?  Almost everyone who uses
Linux uses ext2fs, so I don't expect that you'll run into a lot of people
with experience reading some other operating system's UFS floppies (even
of the same byte order) under Linux.

