Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


I am currently working on a proposal to implement VPN (Virtual
Private Networking) for a user base sufficient to have 100 concurrent
homeworkers logged in and actively running network applications to a
variety of servers and mainframes. The plan is to use a DEC alpha server
for PPTP (Point to Point Tunneling Protocol) which is part of the 
Windows NT package. The user would establish an internet connection
through an ISP, onto the internet to the clients firewall, where this
traffic would pass through a specific port, onto the clients intranet,
to the PPTP server (the DEC alpha running NT), an encryption key
is then sent back to the windows 95 client, and further exchanges 
between the two are encrypted, thusly establishing a secure session.
This session is obviously crypto intensive. I believe the Microsoft
pptp service utilizes a 40 bit encryption standard. 

Now the question.

Given the above constraints, what size DEC Alpha Server would be
recommended. I feel that I am probably looking at a member of the
4100's or even one of the new 8400's.

If you have had experience in this sort of application, or even a
similar experience with using a Proxy Server running that many
concurrent users please respond by e-mail to  egulley@csd.net
or to ta775@msp.psco.com


Ed Gulley
Network System Administrator
IBM Global Network Services

egulley@csd.net , ta775@msp.psco.com
