Joe Pollizzi wrote:
> 
> > >Go with whichever vendor you want, but don't do it on the strength of a
> > >benchmark !
> > >
> >
> > Short of actually bringing in a new system and running your programs
> > side-by-side, how else can you choose among all the different brands and
> > systems other than by benchmarks? And how would you then convince upper
> > management to pay for it, other than by benchmarks?
> 
> I think what Robin was trying to say is don't go by "industry standard"
> benchmarks (such as tpc) as the sole justification for your decision.
> 
> In our experience, the best approach is to develop our own benchmark
> that mirrors or is otherwise comparible to the system's load we expect
> to see on the eventual system. Given such an thing, most reputable
> companies will either provide loaner gear or give access to a benchmarking
> center where you can try your own tests out.

While you may validly (up to a point) compare systems by their TPC
(or Spec) benchmark figures, I think the $/tpm figures are almost
worthless.  If I am buying a system to run a particular application
with a given user population, if I can do that on a $200,000 system
with sub-second response time, why would I be interested in a system
for $500,000 that outperforms it at a lower $/tpm ?  Yes - growth, 
new software etc - but my point is you should compare the costs of
the system that will run _your_ workload at _your_ target performance, 
not the cost of a system that wins the TPC benchmark.  It's not likely
to look anything like the system you're going to buy.

My other point was that choice of vendor shouldn't be simply based
on the published benchmark data.  Given two systems with
adequate performance and similar costs, you need to consider quality
of support, cost of maintenance, reliability of the systems etc.

