:On 11 Dec 1997, Steve Lionel wrote:
:
:> The VAX 10000 and its smaller sibling, the VAX 7000 (platform codenames
:> LAZER/BLAZER) were introduced in July 1992.  They were "mainframe class"
:> systems that could accomodate up to four processors.  They could be upgraded
:> by board-swap to "DEC 10000 AXP" and "DEC 7000 AXP" Alpha-based systems.
:
:Okay, but what was the main difference between the two systems? The
:10000 was a bigger configuration, or something similar? I cannot find
:any difference in the processor/backplane/etc specs... Was this some
:kind of positioning decision?

  The VAX 7000 and the VAX 1000 systems differed electronically only
  in terms of the particular system model identification -- this was
  (obviously) not a significant difference.  Further, OpenVMS will
  effectively treat these two systems as the same.

  The systems differed markedly, however, in terms of the standard
  packaging -- the Blazer configurations usually included additional
  power-related cabinetry -- and in terms of the standard services
  provided.  (One could optionally acquire the extra services and the
  extra power-related capabilities for the VAX 7000.)

