In article <66mk9r$a19$1@main.freenet.hamilton.on.ca>,
Christina Johnson <ac412@freenet.hamilton.on.ca> wrote:
:anybody know how that phone jack looking thing on the
:back of my vt420 with the printer symbol actually connects
:to said printer device?

  The "phone jack looking thing" is the Modified Modular Jack, or MMJ.

  The MMJ pinout is covered in the OpenVMS Frequently Asked Questions
  (FAQ), as are the various available adapters and cables -- there is
  a wealth of other information in the FAQ, as well.

  The FAQ URL is:

    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/vms

  Search for MMJ in the FAQ, and you'll probably learn more about this
  topic than you likely care to know.  :-)

  The tab on the MMJ connection is offset to the side; different from the
  center-mounted tab on the traditional telphone line connection (RJ11).
  This offset prevents cross-connections, and thus prevents any stray 90V
  ring signals from frying the serial line receiver-transmitter circuits.

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
