Stuart Brook <sbrook@concentric.net> writes:
> Parts of LAT are proprietary, so you'll not find in depth protocol info.

o You can get a copy of the patent application (but that is certainly not a
  grant of license to use the patent).

o You can buy one of the "LAT Protocol Specification - Digital Proprietary
  and Confidential" that DEC erroneously sold to anyone who ordered one (I
  have one, it's not for sale, but I think Alan Frisbie has a bunch).

o You can buy the sources or listings of a DEC operating system that includes
  LAT as one of the sources/listings. VMS doesn't, RSTS/E does, I think DEC
  Unix does (Ultrix did, I don't have a source license for DU), I don't know
  about RSX.

o You can buy/rent/borrow a protocol analyzer that captures and decodes LAT
  packets.

