I worked on a utility to read UFS some time back. The issue with Sun is likely
that it's a big-endian machine, so any data item longer than 8 bits gets read
into memory in a different order. Think about reading an inode in, which contains
block numbers on disk. These are wider than 8 bits! They therefore come back
shuffled.

My premise was that there are some magic numbers on BSD FFS disks that can
be recognized so a filesystem can swap inode, directory, and so on, blocks
to get them right, and I was able to get the utility working, at least on the small
filesystem I had. I can't say however whether UFS means precisely BSD FFS or not,
At any rate, filesystems not designed and spec'd to work the same for either "sex"
machine are nontrivial to read.

