In article <348C35E9.978DCB00@aries.scs.uiuc.edu>, Doug McDonald <mcdonald@aries.scs.uiuc.edu> writes:
>DEC has, since the demise of OEM sales of PDP-11s, tried
>to make a "positioned" group of computers such that the lower
>priced ones are crippled in some trivial way.

<SNIP>

>To recap, to succeed big, you have to have a whole line
>of stuff that does not suffer from the "positioning" problem.
>PCs are not like this: there is an obvious "marketing
>position" for various type computers, but any computer
>can in fact be used for any purpose, independent of the
>seller's positioning ideas. 

I agree completely with this analysis.

If people want to run "workstations" as "servers", it is because they think
that the former are up to the job. Digital, on the other hand, insists we
buy more expensive machines to be "servers", whether or not with we need
the extra IO or other features that they offer, and charges more for both
the more capable hardware and the (exact same) software.  They can get away
with this to some extent because if we want that machine to run OpenVMS,
Digital is effectively a monopoly supplier, and they usually just won't
sell extra user licenses for the workstations (maybe that has changed?) 
But these practices and others have made Digital's products terribly
unappealing when compared to other vendors. 

Put another way, software and hardware purchases should be orthogonal.

It would really make me happy if Digital were to:

1.  Separate completely the pricing of hardware and software.  All 
    computers would be sold "bare", with the OS and other software a 
    separate item on the purchase form.

2.  Offer a range of hardware solutions so that the user could scale 
    applications up or down as needed, with the price of the hardware set
    by its capabilities and production costs only.  Range would be rational -
    bigger machines would have more IO capability, more CPUs, and/or more
    in chassis disk storage, but you wouldn't have to buy a particular 
    machine size to run a particular piece of software (unless said 
    software really did require the extra hardware.)

3.  Offer licenses scaling the capabilities of software up and down without
    regard to the underlying hardware.  That is, more user licenses, 
    connections, or whatever, on any size platform, with the price set by
    the software capabilities and production costs only.  Furthermore,
    all software transferable to another machine for no fee. (That is
    transferable, not replicable!)

In this wonderful world (which we will doubtless never see) there would be
none of this constant messing about with various license classes for 
software, or purchasing of unneeded server power when smaller machines
would do the trick.  As with most fixes to Digital, this one mostly
requires a change of mindset and a bit of paperwork (mostly a reduction of
paperwork), but no new technology. 

Just in case it is not sufficiently obvious to Digital management, this is
exactly the pricing structure that exists in the WNT/Intel world, and it is
part of the reason those machines are devouring Digital's installed base. 

Regards,

