Organization: neuron, technology consultants
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


I concur, a BREAK is in fact a mumble number of milliseconds of SPACE on
the line. I do believe that RS232 goes as follows, signals will be
considered in the  MARK "1" state when the voltage is greater than -3VDC
with respect to ground and in the SPACE "0" state when the voltage is
greater than +3VDC with respect to ground. My book seems to indicate a
BREAK is 0.4 seconds.

However, as the BREAK signal can be generated by pressing a specific key
on the typical DIGITAL keyboard, I decided not to go into the extreme
details of BREAK so as not to confuse neophytes.

The circuit driving the line must by definition in the standard drive the
line to either to between -5 to -15 VDC or to +5 to +15 VDC.

rtt


