I'm afraid I must agree with John.  I bought DEC stock 6 years ago (I still
own it) because I like Digital, I like their products and I like the
people who work there.  Unfortunately I'm just a lowly techie - I'm not the
manager who makes the "vision" level decisions.

At my last job anything made by Digital was considered a Bad Thing;
nevermind that the VMS boxes had the best uptime record (VMS 5.4 on a
VAXServer 3400 went 2 years - rebooted only because we had to change the
electrical wiring!) and reliably ran most of the businesses core functions.

At my present job we use Silicon Graphics but there's a  push to move to NT
just because everybody else is, too.  It's funny but when I was a kid my
parents told me not to rush off and do something (smoke, drink, use drugs,
have sex, jump off a cliff, whatever) just because everybody else was. Now
that I'm an adult and a responsible member of society I AM supposed to do
things just because everybody else does.  Huh?!? 

I suppose this ignorance/dislike of Digital on the part of management
exists in part because I, as a technical person, have not done my part to
educate and inform the management people in terms they can understand (no,
giving them a vigorous LART'ing doesn't count, tempting tho' it may be :-). 
They need to know the real cost of all possible solutions to a given
problem and not just what does it cost to get the hardware in the door.
They also need to now how reliable is the vendor - are their promises
trustworthy?  Do they deliver or just emit hot air?  Does the product solve
my problem now, or does it offer nothing more than the the promise of a
possible solution Real Soon Now?

I've wandered around a bit here, but yes - the view of Digital from 
outside must be very different from inside.  When someone in management 
walks past the computer room they don't see Digital on the machine - they
see Compaq or Dell.  When the software on their PC (not AlphaStation)
starts up they see Microsoft.  Their only association with Digital is
either VMS (which they think of as expensive and "legacy" which implies
something old and antiquated and to be done away with as soon as possible -
notice hos legacy is never thought of as "rich heritage of great worth") or
they think of a company that's been selling off body parts and has lost
market share to Microsoft et. al.

For the betterment of our companies and the maintenance of our own
sanity this must change.  But how?  I'm open to suggestions.




