Rob Young wrote:
>         What will that gain you in conjunction with Galaxies is when
>         the BIG IT proposal comes down ... you may be able to get by with
>         spending $5 million instead of $7 million with Oracle + Alpha instead
>         of Oracle + Merced... and get a significant performance boost to
>         boot.

BIG and SERIOUS IT proposales used to be common. The MIS department had
to plan capacity and systems that would cater to the whole company,
thousands of users, many different applications etc.

But these days, I suspect that the growth area is in departmental or
specific-application systems.  So folks don't spend so much  time
planning corporate capacity: they get a request from a department to
install a small server that runs application XYZ. Once done, they go
work on the next request from another department which will also
probably end up requiring yet another PC to be installed as a single-app
server.

And very few of these individual department requests warrant a mainframe
class machine. And in too many instances, a mere glorified 8086 is all
that is needed to house a single application. It also make budgeting
easier since each department has a more "real" invoice for its server
compared to a "virtual" invoice for the share of a corporate mainframe
they may have used in the past.

The market for Alpha and Galaxies is now fairly limited to single
applications that really require teh purpuse of a dedicated "mainframe"
class machine to run that one app.

Ok, perhaps I need to exagerate to make my point, but I do not think
that there is all that much need to buy a chip as fast as the alpha.
There is a need, but it is a niche market.



