Paul Doherty <spam_me_not_pdoherty@dfw.net> writes:

> I would like to hear some discussion on this supposed "power" advantage of an Alpha chip over

> an Intel one...  Everyone I think assumes it is faster because it runs at a higher clock
> rate.  But this is a RISC chip, and thus since all instructions are smaller (less 'complex')
> more need to be run for each function to be completed.  So what are the actual throughtput
> numbers for each type of chip (say PII 300 vs Alpha 533)?
>


(Can't post so I'm emailing you)

You are correct that the performance advantage is not as high as the
difference in clock speeds would suggest, but the 533MHz 21164PC is
slightly faster in integer code and way faster in floating point code
than a Pentium II 300MHz, and it costs less. It also has special
instructions for motion video decoding whereby it blows MMX out of the
water. The next Alpha up (the 21164A) is significantly better again,
e.g. approx three times the fp performance) although admittedly at
higher cost, but this chip hasn't got the MVI set yet. Then there is
the brand new Alpha 21264 chip which is currently being sampled which
is twice the performance of the 21164a at the same clock rate and does
have MVI. Finally Samsung have prototyped the 21264A (shrink of the
first version to 0.25 micron silicon) at 700-800 MHz, that chip offers
approx ten times the performance of the PII at floating point, and
perhaps five times the integer perf. Think of it like this :

performance
^
|                          x Alpha
|                     x
|               x
|          x
|       x       . Intel
|     x    .
|       .
|     .
|   .
|  .
|
------------------------------> cost

Chris

