I'll verify your claims.  We have some fairly intensive Fortran applications
that were migrated over from VAX's.  In the good old days a VAX may take
*days* to complete a run.

We moved this application to MS PowerStation Fortran (Now Digital's Visual
Fortran) and run it on either Intel or Alpha hardware.  On either processor
performance is many many times greater then on a VAX.  On typical Pentium
Pro machines with lots of memory and a good disk subsystem we get about half
the performance we do on our Alpha platforms.  When it comes to floating
point the Alpha's just destroys the Intel x86 processor family.  Floating
point has always been a week spot on Intel platforms as the majority of the
market doesn't performance in this area so Intel doesn't push their designs
heavily in this area.  Because of this there will always be a market for
number crunching machines like the Alpha.  They may not be the answer to put
on your secretaries desktop for running Word, but for the applications that
need them they are truly a wonderful machine.

