Pentium Pro - quite a bit faster and more advanced than Pentium, but
didn't perform impressively on old, 16 bit code.  Also, rather costly to 
produce.  Big mistake on Intels part, with the PII they ensured that it
would run obsolete code fast - on 32bit, it's not significantly faster
than the Pro.

Similar issues *could* apply to Merced, in fact, I'd consider it more
likely that they do, since it's a more radical shift in technology than
the Pro was.

Also, if Intel cedes the x86 market, Cyrix, AMD, IDT et al are ready to
take over.  So it's certainly dangerous for Intel to ramp up Merced
production at the cost of reducing x86.

> Any wholesale push to force the market towards IA64
> may end the same way as IBM's attempt to replace ISA-based
> architectures with MicroChannel.

We see similar things happening with technologies like Slot-1 - people
stick with Socket 7 and AMD K6, since it is just as fast as PII, and a
lot cheaper.  Ditto AGP vs PCI.

