Paul Doherty wrote:
> 
> I would like to hear some discussion on this supposed "power" advantage of an Alpha chip over
> an Intel one...  Everyone I think assumes it is faster because it runs at a higher clock
> rate.  But this is a RISC chip, and thus since all instructions are smaller (less 'complex')
> more need to be run for each function to be completed.  So what are the actual throughtput
> numbers for each type of chip (say PII 300 vs Alpha 533)?

So start a thread on comp.benchmarks - or better yet, search 'em on 
DejaNews. At the chip level, the relative benchmarks used are usually 
SPECint and SPECfp; these do *not* directly translate to faster overall 
system speed, though - it depends on how well the rest of the box is 
made - the memory bandwidth, cache, etc., etc. Depending on your
workload, 
the TPC, STREAM, SPECINT/FPrate, etc. may make more sense.

Nevertheless - the best single-CPU SPECint and SPECfp results 
for Alpha and Intel (from www.specbench.org):

Chip                  System                  SPECFP95      SPECint95
---------------------------------------------------------------------
600 MHz 21164         AlphaServer 4000 5/600      29.20         18.8
Pentium II 300 MHz    SCENIC Celsius 1000          8.56         11.9
---------------------------------------------------------------------



