In article <3486D29A.6D30E3FE@dfw.net>, spam_me_not_pdoherty@dfw.net says...

>I would like to hear some discussion on this supposed "power" advantage of an Alpha chip over
>an Intel one...  Everyone I think assumes it is faster because it runs at a higher clock
>rate.  But this is a RISC chip, and thus since all instructions are smaller (less 'complex')
>more need to be run for each function to be completed.  So what are the actual throughtput
>numbers for each type of chip (say PII 300 vs Alpha 533)?

CISC doesn't always mean fewer instructions.  One of the
advantages seen with the alpha architecture is that you
need not overwrite one of the source registers with each
instruction.  Also it should be noted that 21164s have
two floating point pipelines and can issue a floating
multiply every clock cycle whereas a Pentium II only
has a single floating pipeline and can only issue a
floating multiply every other clock cycle.  With really
tight code on both processors, an alpha can often get
more work done in fewer clock cycles.  The bad news is
that it's quite difficult to write the really tight
code on alpha: compilers certainly don't do it, and not
all that many people are familiar with alpha assembly
language compared to x86 assembly language.




