Actually, you will find that the ALPHA C compiler developed by Digital and
Microsoft, does  a much better job at translating C code than the identical
equivalent available on Intel.  It has to -- the whole RISC design forces
this paradigm.  The ALPHA compiler when generating optimized (release) code
does an incredible job.  We took a small C routine for CRC calculation, and
hand-coded some x86 assembler, and immediately reaped a 500% performance
boost for doing so (this was after 1-2 hrs to do the hand coding) when
compared to the "optimized" assembler generated by the C compiler.  We took
the same code and attempted to write a faster ALPHA assembler version.  Our
first attempt ran at half the speed of the C version.  After about a week of
hand-coding this routine we had achieved a 5-10% performance advantage.  We
ditched it -- rather than maintain some ALPHA assembler that yields such a
weak performance boost.  Take a look someday at ALPHA assembler generated by
the compiler -- it is guaranteed to generate comments such as "oooh" "aaah",
and "wow, that's really clever!".

Regards

--
Paul Bunn, BEI, 425-644-6000
Microsoft MVP - WindowsNT
http://www.ultrabac.com

James Van Buskirk wrote in message
<6698hl$704@dfw-ixnews12.ix.netcom.com>...
>In article <3486D29A.6D30E3FE@dfw.net>, spam_me_not_pdoherty@dfw.net
says...
>
>>I would like to hear some discussion on this supposed "power" advantage of
an Alpha chip over
>>an Intel one...  Everyone I think assumes it is faster because it runs at
a higher clock
>>rate.  But this is a RISC chip, and thus since all instructions are
smaller (less 'complex')
>>more need to be run for each function to be completed.  So what are the
actual throughtput
>>numbers for each type of chip (say PII 300 vs Alpha 533)?
>
>CISC doesn't always mean fewer instructions.  One of the
>advantages seen with the alpha architecture is that you
>need not overwrite one of the source registers with each
>instruction.  Also it should be noted that 21164s have
>two floating point pipelines and can issue a floating
>multiply every clock cycle whereas a Pentium II only
>has a single floating pipeline and can only issue a
>floating multiply every other clock cycle.  With really
>tight code on both processors, an alpha can often get
>more work done in fewer clock cycles.  The bad news is
>that it's quite difficult to write the really tight
>code on alpha: compilers certainly don't do it, and not
>all that many people are familiar with alpha assembly
>language compared to x86 assembly language.
>





