On 4 Dec 1997, Kevin Oberman wrote:

> "Edward J. Russavage" <erussavage@mindspring.com> writes:
> 
> > Howdy,
> > 
> > I am trying to locate when the concept of DECnet routing first appeared,
> > i.e. the concepts of adjacency, level 1 and level 2 routing, etc.  Some
> > company who shall remain nameless thinks that they invented routing
> > concepts relative to OSPF, and thus have presented patents to the OSPF
> > working group stating that the OSPF RFC and systems implementing OSPF
> > infringes these patents.  I believe that some aspects of DECnet routing
> > actually predates these patents.  I would greatly appreciate the help of
> > anyone familiar with DECnet routing to verify the conception dates
> > relative to DECnet, especially concerning routing features.
> 
> The releases of relevance are DECnet Phase III for adaptive routing
> and Phase IV for two level routing. I believe DECnet Phase III hit the 
> streets in 1978 and DECnet Phase IV in 1984. The development of the
> concepts behind them was, of course, older.

DECnet was `pre-implemented' as CSIROnet in the late 70s. Peter Quoddling
posted an note on the date some time ago.

PhI was next node only, PhII had non-adaptive routting. PhIII introduced
adaptive routing. PhIV added the concept of adjacency as part of support
for ethernet. With ethernet a node could be adjacent ( on the same extended
LAN segment, and thus directly conectable ) even if in different areas.
It also allowed multi-area, one ethernet, no router networks, in theory
anyway. Many reasons why you would not want to do it in practice.

~Paul




