From: John Johnstone <johnstone@tridentusa.nspam.com>
Subject: Is IA64 a plus for Digital?
Date: 1997/12/03
Message-ID: <34856BD2.3C71882F@tridentusa.nspam.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 294936047
Organization: Trident Computer Resources
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


Systems running Digital UNIX with the Alpha CPU have had limited
    success in competing against HP.  The fact that the Alpha
    architecture has a performance advantage hasn't allowed Digital to
    substantially improve its competitive position with HP.  The
    advantages of Digital UNIX over HP-UX also have not made a
    significant difference with regard to competing against HP.  This
    same assertion could also be made with other vendors such as Sun. 
    If IA64 will be powering both HP systems and Digital systems, why
    would Digital's use of IA64 improve its position against HP or other
    vendors?  

    Digital has maintained that the Alpha architecture's performance
    advantage allows Digital's systems to be differentiated from IA64
    systems and other CPU designs.  With limited use of Alpha
    architecture techniques, Intel has improved the performance of its
    32-bit Pentium systems to levels that have come significantly close
    to the 64-bit Alpha systems.  If the IA64 CPU will be powering HP's
    systems that compete against Digital's Alpha based systems, that
    means that the IA64 CPU competes against the Alpha CPU.  This means
    that HP will do all that it can to make sure that IA64 or its
    successors becomes superior to Alpha.  Now that Intel can freely
    incorporate Alpha architecture characteristics into its CPU designs,
    isn't there a distinct possibility that the performance of future
    IA64 CPUs or its successors could overtake Alpha CPUs?  In addition
    to HP, won't Intel also have the goal of making IA64 performance
    exceed that of Alpha to gain increased profits through IA64 sales to
    HP and other companies?



