From: William Daley <daley@bulean.enet.dec.com>
Subject: DECnet History
Date: 1997/12/03
Message-ID: <3485D456.1BF1@bulean.enet.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 294966118
Organization: EDS-Scicon
Reply-To: daley@bulean.enet.dec.com
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


The Phase III Digital Network Architecture introduced adaptive routing
in 1978/79. Products were shipped based upong this architecure in 1980.
This architecture provided for a single level network topology of up to
255 nodes.

The Phase IV Digital Network Architecture in May, 1983 introduced the
concept of level 1/Level 2 routing. Digital shipped products based upon
this architecture in 1984. There was support for up to 63 areas with up
to 1023 node per area.

William J. Daley
DECnet/X.25 Engineering Manager
EDS-Scicon - Crosspoint
Lowell, Massachusetts, USA

P.S. - I started working on DECnet in January 1977 during the
development of DECnet Phase II for DECnet-RSX. I transitioned from
Digital to EDS-Scicon in November 1996.



