Joseph B. Gurman wrote:
[snip]
>     But frankly, who would pay the premium for a 64-bit chip as an
> embedded controller (unless, of course, you consider game systems embedded
> controllers)?

   What exactly is this premium?  The increase in chip area due to 64 bit
   integer registers and integer execution unit(s) is quite trivial compared
   to other things like TLBs and L1 caches even for embedded CPUs smaller
   than 50 mm2 in die area.  For many combinations of cost and performance,
   specific 64 bit RISC CPUs beat out all 32 bit alternatives even just for
   running 32 bit code exclusively.

> Digital pretty much narrowed their own market to the high-end when they
> decided to be the first with 64-bit processors.

   IIRC, the 64 bit R4000 beat Alpha to market.  As far as 64 bit chips being
   restricted to the high end, that conclusion is quite erronious.  There are
   several MIPS III ISA (64 bit) CPUs available for well under $30.  A variant
   of the 64 bit R4300i ships in the Nintendo 64 (the whole console retails for
   $150).

> 
