Intel only has the rigths to produce the Alpha chip under licence
from DEC, like Samsung and Mitsubichi does it now.
There is defenately no future in any x86 based architecture, since
this will die with the introduction of IA64.
The IA64 will have absolutely no native software when it arrives,
it will have to compete on equal terms with the other microprocessor
architectures, and this might very well be the big chance for Alpha.
Intel tried a couple years ago to make it big into the RISC market
with the i860 chip, but ended with an embedded version (i960), only
suitable for postscript printers.
Big software-houses like Corel and Quark, have anounced that their
products are being ported to Alpha-NT, and will hit the market in
Q1-98. This will for sure drag a lot of smaller software companies
in the Alpha NT direction very soon.
If the IA64 hits the market in late 99(as promised), it will have
to compete with the Alpha 21364. This means that Intel as usually
