In comp.sys.dec Jerome Fine <jhfine@idirect.com> wrote:
> Perhaps a CQD 220A/TM from CMD would do the
> job.  I believe that the RRD50 is a CD-ROM reader.
> If anyone out there knows, we would appreciate some
> help.

  Yes, the RRD50 is the original CD-ROM reader. It's a LMS drive with a 
screwball serial interface. The RRD40 is a caddy version of this drive,
with the same screwball interface. DEC also purchased some adapter boards
from LMS to make the RRD40 work with the SCSI bus in the Infoserver 100.

  The controller cards are the KRQ50 (for Q-bus) and the KRP50 (for the
PC ISA bus).

  All of this stuff is horribly obsolete and slow (the RRD50 is *slower*
than a 1X CD-ROM). I'd strongly suggest getting an RRD42 (if you're con-
necting it to a box with DEC SCSI, like the VAXstation 3100, and care
about being able to boot VMS from it) or a generic SCSI CD-ROM (if you
have a non-DEC controller or don't care about booting on a 3100).

