X-Deja-AN: 294540406
References: <65vi27$4mb@nr1.calgary.istar.net>
Organization: U.S. Design
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Brad Hawken wrote:
> 
> I've written some applications in C on Unix and now I have to port them to
> the Vax (which I know nothing about I'm afraid).
> 
> Mostly I'm done however I have run into the problem on the vax where once a
> file has been opened using the system routines open or creat I cannot access
> these files with with other calls to open from within the same process or
> even read the files externally (i.e. with TYPE).  This is required since
> this application is a logging API that will append, recreate, write, etc to
> a file from within any other application.
> 
> My question is, what "keywords = what values" should be used in order to
> make the newly created file sharable (I think that is the vax lingo).
> Essentially creat(e) the file so that once a file is created calling open()
> again to retrieve a new file descriptor doesn't give the error
> "65535 file currently locked by another user"
> 
> Thanks in advance for any help on this.

If you have access to the DEC C Run-time Manual, the "keyword=value"
information is listed in the "creat" call.  You may want to try the
following keyword(s):

     "shr=put"   - allows users to write
     "shr=upd"   - allows users to update
     "shr=get"   - allows users to read
     "shr=upi"   - allows one or more writers

I have not used the last option, but it sounds reasonable.  There may be
other relevant options...

You will want to get the DEC C Runtime Manual and possibly the VMS
System Services Manual and perhaps the RMS Manual.

Chuck McCrobie

-- 
ALL OPINIONS DO NOT NECESSARILY REFLECT THOSE OF MY EMPLOYER.



