Someone was kind enough to inform me via email that the Matrox
Millenium II video card is supported under the most recent XFree86
distribution after I had already installed it.  That prompted me to
post this message to let others know.

I repartitioned my hard disk so that about 500MB was for /home,
500MB was for /usr/local, 900MB was for / and 100MB was for an MSDOS
FAT partition that I could mount in Linux.  I downloaded the appropriate
RedHat packages on my IBM PC compatible computer and transferred them
in individual sliced-up pieces with floppy disks over to the 100MB MSDOS
partition of my Alpha computer.  The filenames on the MSDOS partition had
to be shorter than the original names, so I had to write down the original
names and mv them to different names on Linux.  I have not figured out
yet how to enable modular parallel port Zip drive support on the Alpha, but
having Zip drive access would make my life a lot easier until I get a
web browser for X Windows so that I can download directly to Linux.

The most recent distributed version of XFree86 is 3.3.1 (versus 3.3-1).  It
is available from various sites, among them the ftp mirror sites listed
along the appropriate links from http://www.redhat.com in the
*.rpm packages for the Alpha machines.  The minimal packages that you need
to get X windows running for the Matrox Millenium II on an Alpha
machine under Linux are:

     XFree86-3.3.1-1.alpha.rpm
     XFree86-SVGA-3.3.1-1.alpha.rpm
     XF86Setup-3.3.1-1.alpha.rpm
     XFree86-libs-3.3.1-1.alpha.rpm
     XFree86-100dpi-fonts-3.3.1-1.alpha.rpm
     XFree86-75dpi-fonts-3.3.1-1.alpha.rpm

I am not sure if the XF86Setup program is really necessary.  If you
install it, then it has to be installed with the rpm -i --force command.
To run the XF86Setup program, you have to either copy the XF86_SVGA
server to XF86_VGA16 or create a link (gleaned from an AltaVista search):

     cd /usr/X11R6/bin
     ln -s XF86_SVGA XF86_VGA16

This XF86Setup program expects XF86_VGA16 to be present, but XF86_VGA16
presently does not exist for the Alpha platform.  I found that I could
not run XF86Setup from a plain command prompt to completion.  XF86Setup
would tell me that it was switching to graphics mode, but then it would
just freeze, so I had to press CTRL C to break out of it.  X windows
seems to expect /etc/X11 to be in the path, to access the X file, which
is a link to /usr/X11R6/bin/XF86_SVGA, so I added /etc/X11 to the path by
editing the /etc/profile file, logging out, and then logging back in.
I found that I was able to run X windows without running XF86Setup.  I
used the startx command to enter X windows.  I could then run XF86Setup,
but it would lock up when I told it to save the changes.  So, I ended
up manually editing the /etc/X11/XConfiguration or some similarly named
file so that I could specify my monitor as a multisync monitor and
switch graphics modes with CTRL ALT + or CTRL ALT - keystrokes.

Such are the obstacles you have to overcome, and that is part of the fun
if you like to explore an emerging frontier.

http://www.matrox.alloy.net/eng/index.html is apparently the home page
for the Matrox Millenium support effort for XFree86 and contains
information about the Matrox Millenium II support in XFree86 3.3.1, 
geared towards IBM PC compatible versions of Linux, but some of the
information is relevant to Alpha platforms.

-Ed L



