In article <01bcffe6$1b46d880$0a3763c3@lt0001>, "Ali Chowdhury" <ali.chowdhury@btinternet.com> wrote:
>        
>I have been using the AlphaStation as a VMS machine. Now I would like to
>load NT Server 4.0 to use as Exchange or SMS Server.
>
>I have tried setting OS_TYPE to NT and CONSOLE to GRAPHICS but could not
>get into menu to load NT.
>
>Could anyone advise ? Do I need any firmware upgrade ?
>
>Thanks

        From our internal documentation (edited slightly) :

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#2 Convert an AlphaStation 255 from OpenVMS to Windows NT

+ Following hardware is required:

  - An Alpha-based Windows NT system (usage only).
  - 2 floppies (1.44 MB, 3.5"), one of which must be FAT-formatted.
  - A PCI video card, capable of displaying VGA (640 x 480 x 16 colors).
  - A video monitor, capable of displaying VGA (640 x 480 x 16 colors).
  - A keyboard, to be connected directly to the system (keyboard slot at the
    back of the system case). It is possible that the keyboard, connected to
    the VT terminal of the VMS system to be converted to NT, can be used.

+ Obtain the latest AlphaBIOS firmware for AlphaStation 255.

  At the time of writing, this latest version was v5.12, stored in the file
  "ab255v512.zip" which can be found on
  ''ftp://ftp.digital.com/private/fwaxp/''.

+ On the Alpha-based Windows NT system, create a temporary directory, move
  (or copy) this file into it and use the utility "pkunzip.exe" or
  "ntunzip.exe" to extract the archive.


  The following files should be created :

        fwupdate.exe
        lx3ab.cmp
        lx3ab.rom
        makeboot.exe
        readme.1st

+ Insert the empty floppy into the floppy drive (here assumed to be "a:")
  and type 

        makeboot -f lx3ab.cmp -d a: [KEY RETURN]

  Press [KEY Y] to continue when the makeboot utility asks

        Continue ? [Yes/No (default)]

  This will create an "AlphaBIOS-bootable floppy".

+ Insert the FAT-formatted (empty) floppy into the floppy drive, and copy
  the files "lx3ab.rom" and "fwupdate.exe" onto it.

  This floppy will be called the "AlphaBIOS-update floppy".

+ Reboot the VMS system to be converted to WNT. Press [KEY CTRL][KEY P] or
  [KEY CTRL][KEY C] during the boot sequence to arrive at the ''>>>'' prompt.

  Here, type the following lines (without the ''>>>'') and press [KEY RETURN]
  after each line :

        >>> set os_type NT
        >>> set console graphics
        >>> set auto_action boot

+ Shut down the system. Disconnect the VT terminal. Open the system case.
  Insert the PCI video card into an empty PCI slot. Connect the monitor.
  Connect the keyboard. Set the motherboard DIP switch pack switches labeled
  "Config0" and "Config1" to OFF and ON, respectively (this will cause the
  system to boot from the floppy drive on power-up). Close the system case.

+ Insert the AlphaBIOS-bootable floppy into the floppy drive and power up
  the system; AlphaBIOS will boot from the floppy.

  During the AlphaBIOS boot sequence, warnings informing that portions of
  the NVRAM (non-volatile RAM) are inconsistent can be issued. Acknowledge
  by pressing [KEY RETURN].

  Press [KEY F2] after the memory test to open the AlphaBIOS Setup.

  If [KEY F2] was not pressed in time, the system will inform that no boot
  partitions (or operating systems) could be found. Pressing [KEY F2] will
  display the AlphaBIOS Setup.

+ Remove the AlphaBIOS-bootable floppy from the floppy drive and insert
  the AlphaBIOS-update floppy.

  Then (in the AlphaBIOS Setup screen) select "Upgrade AlphaBIOS" and press
  [KEY RETURN]. The AlphaBIOS will now be written in the system's NVRAM.

  It is possible that the system issues a warning ("invalid file type" or
  something similar). Ignore this warning (press [KEY RETURN] to continue).

  After a while, the system will display the current (should be v5.12) and
  the new (also v5.12) AlphaBIOS version. Press [KEY RETURN] to continue.

  Next, a message will be displayed, informing that the upgrade is about
  to start (including a warning not to power down the system during the
  upgrade). Press [KEY F10] to continue.

  During the upgrade procedure, the system may reboot. If so, proceed as in
  step 7) to get to the AlphaBIOS Setup.

+ Power down the system. Remove the floppy from the floppy drive. Open the
  system case. Set the motherboard DIP switch pack switches labeled
  "Config0" and "Config1" both to OFF (this will cause the system to boot
  from a hard disk on power-up). Close the system case.

+ The system is now ready for Windows NT.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

        CU, JDO


