In article <3483084d.77536982@news.wizards.net>, SpamTrap@127.0.0.1 writes:
|>Hi, folks,
|>
|>	I've inherited a VAXServer 3100 (and I mean a SERVER, not the same as
|>a VAXStation 3100 although they do look somewhat similar). The power
|>supply appears to have a problem (the green LED on its front blinks at
|>about one-second intervals and the system won't boot), and I lack a
|>schematic with which to dig in and fix it.

The VAXserver 3100 you have could have been a VAXstation 3100 with a different
badge on the front or a MicroVAX 3100 with the server ROMs. Normally I would
ask you to post the console output to distinguish them (KA41-? = MicroVAX,
KA42-? = VAXstation) but that is going to be a little hard in your case :-)

The VAXstation powersupply is H7821-00, I forget the MicroVAX one but it's
a similar size. I would guess that what is happening is that something is
periodically overloading the PSU and causing it to crowbar. Try disconnecting
everything from the PSU and powering up: the light won't come on but the two
integral fans should turn. Now try connecting to the motherboard with all
options and external connections removed: if the beast powers up OK then add
options back to find out which one is causing the problem (mine was a mouse!).
Otherwise you have a dead m/b or PSU.

You may not find a PSU on its own but the VAXstation 3100 series all use the
same PSU (as did some of the DECstation MIPS range).

|>
|>	Also, where would I tie in a console terminal on this unit?
|>

Plug it into the printer port. The small switch at the back (S3) needs to
be up.
